  v_plan constant jsonb := $audit_plan${"generatedBy":"scripts/audit/apply-audit-fixes.ts","problems":[{"patch_id":"R2-G07","problem_id":"3c327f82-85da-4467-9209-bc8dfc5256ff","from_version_id":"582baed1-600b-4016-9e6e-f5c426b293ea","expect":{"options":["In doing so, however","In doing so","Despite doing so","In doing so, or"],"explanation":"이 문장은 앞서 설명한 소금 유약 기법 사용이라는 행위와 그로 인한 결과(전통 보존)를 자연스럽게 연결해야 한다. \"In doing so\"는 순접의 결과 관계를 정확히 전달하는 표현이다. \"In doing so, however\"는 앞뒤 내용이 대조 관계가 아닌데도 불필요한 역접어를 추가해 논리를 왜곡하고, \"Despite doing so\"는 양보 관계를 만들어 오히려 결과가 방해받았다는 잘못된 인과를 암시하며, \"In doing so, or\"는 등위접속사 \"or\"를 절 사이에 부적절하게 삽입해 문법적으로 성립하지 않는다. 따라서 정답은 B번이다.","explanation_en":"This sentence must naturally connect the act of using the salt-glaze technique described earlier with its result (preserving the tradition). 'In doing so' conveys that cause-and-result relationship precisely. 'In doing so, however' adds an unnecessary contrast word even though the ideas before and after are not in contrast, distorting the logic. 'Despite doing so' creates a concessive relationship that implies the result was hindered, a false causal link. 'In doing so, or' inserts the coordinating conjunction 'or' improperly between clauses and is ungrammatical. So the answer is B."},"set":{"options":["In doing so, and","In doing so","In doing so, which","In doing so, or"],"explanation":"이 문장은 빈칸 뒤에 \", these potters preserved ...\"라는 완전한 독립절이 이어지므로, 빈칸에는 이 절 앞에 놓이는 삽입 수식어구가 와야 한다. \"In doing so\"는 앞 문장의 행위를 받는 완결된 수식어구로 뒤 독립절과 콤마로 자연스럽게 이어진다. \"In doing so, and\"와 \"In doing so, or\"는 등위접속사가 콤마 뒤에 매달려 연결할 대상이 없고, \"In doing so, which\"는 관계대명사가 이끄는 절이 완성되지 않아 모두 비문이다. 따라서 정답은 B이다.","explanation_en":"After the blank comes a complete independent clause, \", these potters preserved ...,\" so the blank must hold an introductory phrase placed before that clause. \"In doing so\" is a complete introductory phrase that picks up the action of the previous sentence and connects to the clause with a comma. \"In doing so, and\" and \"In doing so, or\" leave a coordinating conjunction hanging with nothing to join, and \"In doing so, which\" starts a relative clause that is never completed, so all three are ungrammatical. So the answer is B."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.238Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-G07","fromVersionId":"582baed1-600b-4016-9e6e-f5c426b293ea","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-G08","problem_id":"ef903982-122d-41bf-a83c-9de904e8c159","from_version_id":"341d6652-9a52-4001-933b-757aee8ce49a","expect":{"options":["Using specialized underwater cameras, that could capture faint light in near-total darkness,","Using specialized underwater cameras that could capture faint light in near-total darkness,","Using specialized underwater cameras that could capture faint light in near-total darkness","She used specialized underwater cameras that could capture faint light in near-total darkness, and"],"explanation":"빈칸 뒤에는 \"she was able to document...\"라는 완전한 독립절이 이어진다. 따라서 빈칸은 이 절을 수식하는 분사구(-ing로 시작하는 종속 수식어구)여야 하며, 분사구 끝에는 콤마가 필요하다. B는 이 조건을 만족한다. A는 \"cameras\"와 \"that\" 사이에 불필요한 콤마를 넣어 제한적 관계절을 잘못 끊는다. C는 분사구 뒤에 콤마가 없어 뒤 독립절과의 경계가 불분명해진다(문장 경계 오류). D는 \"She used...cameras...in near-total darkness, and she was able to document\"처럼 두 개의 독립절을 and로 연결하는 형태가 되어 원래 문장의 주어(she)가 이미 뒤에 나오므로 주어가 중복되어 문장이 성립하지 않는다.","explanation_en":"After the blank comes a complete independent clause, \"she was able to document...\". Therefore, the blank must be a participial phrase (a subordinate modifying phrase beginning with -ing) that modifies this clause, and a comma is needed at the end of the participial phrase. B satisfies this condition. A inserts an unnecessary comma between \"cameras\" and \"that,\" wrongly breaking a restrictive relative clause. C has no comma after the participial phrase, so the boundary with the following independent clause is unclear (a sentence-boundary error). D joins two independent clauses with and, as in \"She used...cameras...in near-total darkness, and she was able to document,\" but the sentence's subject (she) already appears afterward, so the subject is duplicated and the sentence does not work."},"set":{"options":["Using specialized underwater cameras, that could capture faint light in near-total darkness,","Using specialized underwater cameras that could capture faint light in near-total darkness,","Using specialized underwater cameras that could capture faint light in near-total darkness","She used specialized underwater cameras that could capture faint light in near-total darkness,"],"explanation":"빈칸 뒤에는 \"she was able to document...\"라는 완전한 독립절이 이어진다. 따라서 빈칸은 이 절을 수식하는 분사구(-ing로 시작하는 수식어구)여야 하고, 분사구 끝에는 콤마가 필요하다. B는 이 조건을 만족한다. A는 \"cameras\"와 \"that\" 사이에 불필요한 콤마를 넣어 제한적 관계절을 잘못 끊는다. C는 분사구 뒤에 콤마가 없어 뒤 독립절과의 경계가 불분명해진다. D는 독립절 \"She used ... darkness,\"와 독립절 \"she was able to document ...\"를 접속사 없이 콤마만으로 이어 콤마 스플라이스가 된다. 따라서 정답은 B이다.","explanation_en":"After the blank comes a complete independent clause, \"she was able to document...\". The blank must therefore be a participial phrase (beginning with -ing) that modifies this clause, and a comma is needed at the end of the phrase. B satisfies this condition. A inserts an unnecessary comma between \"cameras\" and \"that,\" wrongly breaking a restrictive relative clause. C has no comma after the participial phrase, so the boundary with the independent clause that follows is unclear. D joins two independent clauses, \"She used ... darkness,\" and \"she was able to document ...,\" with only a comma, which is a comma splice. So the answer is B."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.239Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-G08","fromVersionId":"341d6652-9a52-4001-933b-757aee8ce49a","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-G10a","problem_id":"197fb7f1-4641-4965-ac6f-03a91db8088b","from_version_id":"4568ff47-01ff-4cdf-850b-4ae378e85a80","expect":{"options":["and thereby","and, thereby","and, thereby,","and; thereby"],"explanation":"이 문장은 'warmer water'가 주어인 하나의 절 안에서 'speeds embryonic development'와 '(thereby) shortens the incubation period'라는 두 개의 동사구가 접속사 'and'로 연결된 구조다. 'thereby'는 부사로서 뒤의 동사 'shortens'를 수식할 뿐, 독립된 절을 이끌지 않으므로 그 앞뒤에 쉼표나 세미콜론을 넣을 필요가 없다. B는 'and,' 뒤에 불필요한 쉼표를 넣어 'thereby'를 마치 삽입어구처럼 분리하고, C는 앞뒤로 쉼표를 둘 다 넣어 과도하게 분리하며, D는 세미콜론을 써서 마치 'thereby shortens...'가 독립절인 것처럼 취급하는데 이는 세미콜론이 요구하는 절 경계 규칙을 위반한다. 따라서 아무 문장부호도 없이 'and thereby'로 자연스럽게 연결하는 A가 정답이다.","explanation_en":"This sentence has a structure in which two verb phrases, \"speeds embryonic development\" and \"(thereby) shortens the incubation period,\" are joined by the conjunction \"and\" inside a single clause whose subject is \"warmer water.\" \"Thereby\" is an adverb that merely modifies the following verb \"shortens\" and does not introduce an independent clause, so no comma or semicolon is needed before or after it. B puts an unnecessary comma after \"and,\" setting off \"thereby\" as if it were a parenthetical. C puts commas on both sides and over-separates it. D uses a semicolon, treating \"thereby shortens...\" as if it were an independent clause, which violates the clause-boundary rule that a semicolon requires. So A, which connects them smoothly as \"and thereby\" with no punctuation, is the answer."},"set":{"options":["and thereby","and, thereby","and thereby,","and; thereby"],"explanation":"이 문장은 'warmer water'가 주어인 하나의 절 안에서 'speeds embryonic development'와 'thereby shortens the incubation period'라는 두 개의 동사구가 접속사 'and'로 연결된 구조다. 'thereby'는 뒤의 동사 'shortens'를 수식하는 부사일 뿐 독립절을 이끌지 않으므로 앞뒤에 문장부호가 필요 없다. B는 'and,' 뒤에만 쉼표를 두어 짝이 없는 쉼표가 되고, C는 'thereby' 뒤에만 쉼표를 두어 역시 짝이 없는 쉼표가 되며, D는 세미콜론으로 동사구를 독립절처럼 분리해 세미콜론의 절 경계 규칙을 어긴다. 따라서 아무 문장부호 없이 'and thereby'로 잇는 A가 정답이다.","explanation_en":"This sentence has two verb phrases, \"speeds embryonic development\" and \"thereby shortens the incubation period,\" joined by \"and\" inside a single clause whose subject is \"warmer water.\" \"Thereby\" is an adverb that modifies the following verb \"shortens\" and does not introduce an independent clause, so no punctuation is needed on either side of it. B puts a comma only after \"and,\" which leaves an unpaired comma. C puts a comma only after \"thereby,\" which is likewise unpaired. D uses a semicolon to separate a verb phrase as if it were an independent clause, which breaks the clause-boundary rule for semicolons. So A, which joins the phrases as \"and thereby\" with no punctuation, is the answer."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.239Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-G10a","fromVersionId":"4568ff47-01ff-4cdf-850b-4ae378e85a80","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-G10b","problem_id":"8b49717e-5b2f-45ea-8a6f-e3478b914095","from_version_id":"053a55ae-bd38-4429-97cf-0899444361e5","expect":{"options":["which had reviewed over two hundred community submissions","having reviewed over two hundred community submissions","reviewing over two hundred community submissions had","having reviewed over two hundred community submissions and residents' letters carefully"],"explanation":"문두의 분사구는 뒤에 오는 문장의 주어를 수식해야 하므로, 콤마 뒤에 바로 \"the planning committee\"가 나와 분사구의 논리적 주어와 실제 문장 주어가 일치해야 한다. \"having reviewed over two hundred community submissions\"는 이 조건을 만족시키며, 문장의 유일한 정형 동사인 \"approved\"와 자연스럽게 연결된다. 반면 \"which had reviewed...\"는 관계절로 콤마 뒤에 명사(주어)가 없어 문장이 두 개의 정형 동사 구조 없이 붕 뜨게 되고, 결과적으로 분사구가 무엇을 수식하는지 불분명해지는 댕글링 모디파이어 오류를 일으킨다. \"reviewing...had\"는 동사 형태가 뒤섞여 비문이 된다. 마지막 선택지는 분사구 자체는 문법적으로 옳지만 \"residents' letters carefully\"라는 수식어가 어색하게 붙어 분사구의 논리적 연결이 흐트러지며 주어와의 관계가 모호해진다.","explanation_en":"The participial phrase at the start must modify the subject of the sentence that follows, so right after the comma 'the planning committee' must appear, and the logical subject of the participial phrase must match the actual subject of the sentence. 'Having reviewed over two hundred community submissions' meets this condition and connects naturally to 'approved,' the only finite verb in the sentence. In contrast, 'which had reviewed...' is a relative clause, and with no noun (subject) right after the comma, the sentence is left hanging without a structure of two finite verbs, and the result is a dangling modifier error, because it is unclear what the phrase modifies. 'Reviewing...had' mixes verb forms and makes the sentence ungrammatical. The last choice is grammatically fine as a participial phrase itself, but the modifier 'residents' letters carefully' is attached awkwardly, which muddles the logical connection of the participial phrase and makes its relationship to the subject unclear."},"set":{"options":["which having reviewed over two hundred community submissions","having reviewed over two hundred community submissions","reviewing over two hundred community submissions had","having reviewed over two hundred community submissions and"],"explanation":"빈칸 뒤에는 \", the planning committee ultimately approved ...\"라는 완전한 문장이 이어지므로 빈칸에는 이 문장 앞에 놓이는 삽입 수식어구가 들어가야 하고, 그 수식어구의 논리적 주어는 뒤의 주어 \"the planning committee\"여야 한다. \"having reviewed over two hundred community submissions\"는 완료분사구로 이 조건을 만족하며 문법적으로 완결된다. \"which having reviewed...\"는 관계대명사 뒤에 정동사 없이 분사만 와서 비문이다. \"reviewing ... had\"는 동사 형태가 뒤섞여 비문이다. 마지막 선택지는 \"submissions and\" 뒤에 곧바로 쉼표가 이어져 접속사 뒤에 연결할 대상이 없다. 따라서 정답은 B이다.","explanation_en":"After the blank comes a complete sentence, \", the planning committee ultimately approved ...,\" so the blank must hold an introductory modifier whose logical subject is \"the planning committee.\" \"Having reviewed over two hundred community submissions\" is a perfect participial phrase that does this and is grammatically complete. \"Which having reviewed...\" puts a participle after a relative pronoun with no finite verb, which is ungrammatical. \"Reviewing ... had\" mixes verb forms and is ungrammatical. The last choice ends with \"and\" directly before the comma, so the conjunction connects nothing. So the answer is B."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.239Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-G10b","fromVersionId":"053a55ae-bd38-4429-97cf-0899444361e5","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-G11a","problem_id":"b7c3a411-636e-4408-82ca-2c39be73e605","from_version_id":"c17904b9-df75-486e-8652-0b2eab139daf","expect":{"options":["meanwhile","consequently","she","having achieved this"],"explanation":"이 문장은 이미 \"and\"로 두 절을 연결하고 있으므로 빈칸에는 인과 관계를 보여주는 부사 \"consequently\"가 들어가 자연스럽게 이어진다. \"meanwhile\"은 동시성을 나타내는 부사라 정밀한 기술을 배운 것과 상업적 성공 사이의 인과 관계를 왜곡한다. \"she\"는 이미 \"and\" 뒤에 주어 \"she\"가 따로 나오므로 이를 넣으면 주어가 중복되어 문장 구조가 어색해진다. \"having achieved this\"는 분사구로 완전한 절을 만들지 못해 \"and\" 뒤에 놓일 때 문장이 미완성된다. 따라서 정답은 B번이다.","explanation_en":"This sentence already joins two clauses with \"and,\" so the adverb \"consequently,\" which shows a cause-and-effect relationship, fits naturally in the blank. \"Meanwhile\" is an adverb of simultaneity, so it distorts the causal relationship between learning precise techniques and achieving commercial success. \"She\" would duplicate the subject, since the subject \"she\" already appears separately after \"and,\" which makes the sentence structure awkward. \"Having achieved this\" is a participial phrase that cannot form a complete clause, so the sentence is left unfinished when it is placed after \"and.\" Therefore, the answer is B."},"set":{"options":["consequently;","consequently","consequently:","consequent"],"explanation":"이 문장은 \"and\"로 두 절을 이으므로 빈칸에는 뒤의 \"she became ...\" 절을 수식하는 부사가 와야 한다. \"consequently\"는 부사로서 \"and consequently she became ...\"처럼 자연스럽게 이어진다. \"consequently;\"와 \"consequently:\"는 등위접속사 \"and\" 바로 뒤에 세미콜론이나 콜론을 두어 절 경계를 부당하게 만들고, \"consequent\"는 형용사라 절을 수식하는 부사 자리에 올 수 없다. 따라서 정답은 B이다.","explanation_en":"This sentence joins two clauses with \"and,\" so the blank must hold an adverb that modifies the clause \"she became ...\" that follows. \"Consequently\" is an adverb and fits as \"and consequently she became ....\" \"Consequently;\" and \"consequently:\" put a semicolon or colon directly after the coordinating conjunction \"and,\" which creates an illegitimate clause boundary, and \"consequent\" is an adjective, so it cannot sit in a position that modifies the clause. So the answer is B."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.239Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-G11a","fromVersionId":"c17904b9-df75-486e-8652-0b2eab139daf","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-G11b","problem_id":"8d4393d8-8f93-40d6-9122-0f1b16ac7f4e","from_version_id":"8f39fb20-c309-4be8-bcca-944c4271621b","expect":{"options":["the obstacles that had limited her research were overcome, and","despite these obstacles Noether persisted in her research, so","Noether persisted in her research despite these obstacles, and","Noether's persistence in her research, despite these obstacles, meant that"],"explanation":"이 문장은 'Having spent years working without pay...'로 시작하는 분사구문으로 시작하는데, 이 분사구는 문법적으로 그 뒤에 바로 나오는 주어를 수식해야 한다. 즉 '급여 없이 오랜 세월 일한' 주체는 노에터 자신이어야 하므로, 빈칸 다음에는 반드시 'Noether'가 주어로 와야 한다. C는 'Noether persisted in her research despite these obstacles, and'로 시작하여 분사구가 정확히 노에터를 수식하도록 하고, 등위접속사 'and'로 두 개의 독립절('노에터가 인내했다'와 '그녀의 통찰이 인정받았다')을 자연스럽게 연결한다. A는 분사구가 'the obstacles'를 수식하게 만들어 버려, 마치 장애물 자체가 급여 없이 일했다는 비논리적인 문장이 된다(현수 수식어 오류). B는 'despite these obstacles Noether persisted in her research, so'로 쓰면 분사구 뒤에 다시 'despite'로 장애물을 반복 언급하며 어색하게 종속절 구조를 만들고, 결과를 나타내는 'so'는 두 사건 사이의 관계를 지나치게 인과적으로 왜곡한다(단순히 시간 순서상 나중에 인정받았다는 것이지, 그녀의 인내가 인정의 직접적 원인이라는 논리적 결과 관계가 아니다). D는 'Noether's persistence in her research, despite these obstacles, meant that'로 시작하면 분사구 뒤에 오는 주어가 'Noether'가 아니라 'Noether's persistence'라는 추상명사가 되어, 분사구가 노에터라는 사람이 아니라 그녀의 '인내'라는 개념을 수식하게 되는 현수 수식어 오류가 발생한다. 따라서 정답은 C이다.","explanation_en":"The sentence begins with the participial phrase 'Having spent years working without pay...', and grammatically that phrase must modify the subject that comes right after it. The one who 'spent years working without pay' has to be Noether herself, so 'Noether' must be the subject after the blank. C begins 'Noether persisted in her research despite these obstacles, and', so the participial phrase correctly modifies Noether, and the coordinating conjunction 'and' links the two independent clauses (Noether persisted, and her insights were recognized) naturally. A makes the phrase modify 'the obstacles', which produces the illogical sentence that the obstacles themselves worked without pay (a dangling modifier). B, 'despite these obstacles Noether persisted in her research, so', repeats the obstacles with 'despite' after the participial phrase, which makes the structure awkward, and the result word 'so' distorts the relationship between the two events into a causal one (the recognition simply came later in time; her persistence is not the direct logical cause of the recognition). D, 'Noether's persistence in her research, despite these obstacles, meant that', makes the subject after the participial phrase the abstract noun 'Noether's persistence' rather than 'Noether', so the phrase modifies the concept of her persistence instead of the person, which is a dangling modifier error. Therefore the answer is C."},"set":{"options":["the obstacles that had limited her research were overcome, and","her persistence in her research despite these obstacles, so","Noether persisted in her research despite these obstacles, and","Noether's persistence in her research, despite these obstacles, meant that"],"explanation":"이 문장은 'Having spent years working without pay...'라는 분사구문으로 시작하는데, 분사구문의 의미상 주어는 콤마 뒤에 오는 문장의 주어와 같아야 한다. '급여 없이 오랜 세월 일한' 주체는 노에터 자신이므로 빈칸 뒤 주어는 'Noether'여야 한다. C는 'Noether persisted in her research despite these obstacles, and'로 시작해 분사구가 정확히 노에터를 수식하고, 등위접속사 'and'로 두 독립절을 자연스럽게 연결한다. A는 분사구가 'the obstacles'를, B는 'her persistence'를, D는 'Noether's persistence'를 수식하게 되어 '급여 없이 일한' 주체가 사람이 아닌 대상이 되는 현수 수식어 오류다. 따라서 정답은 C이다.","explanation_en":"The sentence begins with the participial phrase \"Having spent years working without pay...,\" and such a phrase must modify the subject that comes right after it. The one who worked without pay has to be Noether herself, so \"Noether\" must be the subject after the blank. C begins \"Noether persisted in her research despite these obstacles, and,\" so the phrase correctly modifies Noether, and \"and\" joins two independent clauses. In A the phrase modifies \"the obstacles,\" in B \"her persistence,\" and in D \"Noether's persistence,\" so the one who \"worked without pay\" would be a thing instead of a person, which is a dangling modifier. So the answer is C."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.239Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-G11b","fromVersionId":"8f39fb20-c309-4be8-bcca-944c4271621b","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-G12","problem_id":"65748760-4afd-4506-9289-a5e1f9af6f9e","from_version_id":"ec68b76c-3529-4c1b-83a8-3a3582b6a94e","expect":{"correct_index":2,"passage":"A community orchestra's percussion section includes a timpanist, a xylophone player, and a drummer, each of whom must tune ______ instrument before every rehearsal to match the ensemble's pitch.","explanation":"이 문장에서 \"each of whom\"의 \"each\"는 단수 취급되는 대명사이므로, 뒤따르는 소유격 대명사도 단수여야 한다. \"his or her\"는 사람을 가리키는 단수 소유격으로 \"each\"와 정확히 수 일치한다. \"their\"는 복수형이라 단수인 \"each\"와 어긋나고, \"its\"는 사람이 아닌 사물을 가리키는 대명사라 세 연주자를 가리키기에 부적절하며, \"one's\"는 특정하지 않은 일반적인 사람을 가리키는 표현으로 앞서 언급된 특정 세 사람(each of whom) 중 한 명을 가리키는 문맥에 맞지 않는다. 따라서 정답은 C번이다.","explanation_en":"In this sentence, \"each\" in \"each of whom\" is a pronoun treated as singular, so the possessive pronoun that follows must also be singular. \"His or her\" is a singular possessive that refers to people and agrees exactly in number with \"each.\" \"Their\" is plural and clashes with the singular \"each,\" \"its\" refers to things rather than people and is inappropriate for the three performers, and \"one's\" refers to an unspecified person in general, which does not fit a context pointing to one of the three specific people mentioned earlier (each of whom). Therefore, the answer is C."},"set":{"correct_index":0,"passage":"A community orchestra's percussion section includes a timpanist, a xylophone player, and a drummer, all of whom must tune ______ instruments before every rehearsal to match the ensemble's pitch.","explanation":"이 문장에서 \"all of whom\"은 앞의 세 연주자(timpanist, xylophone player, drummer)를 가리키는 복수 선행사이고, 뒤의 명사도 복수형 \"instruments\"이다. 따라서 소유격 대명사도 복수여야 하므로 \"their\"가 정답이다. \"its\"는 사물을 가리키는 단수형이라 사람들을 가리킬 수 없고, \"his or her\"와 \"one's\"는 단수형이라 복수 선행사·복수 명사와 수가 맞지 않는다. 따라서 정답은 A이다.","explanation_en":"In this sentence \"all of whom\" refers to the three players (the timpanist, the xylophone player, and the drummer), a plural antecedent, and the noun that follows is the plural \"instruments.\" The possessive pronoun must therefore be plural, so \"their\" is correct. \"Its\" is singular and refers to things, not people, and \"his or her\" and \"one's\" are singular, so they do not agree with the plural antecedent. So the answer is A."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.240Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-G12","fromVersionId":"ec68b76c-3529-4c1b-83a8-3a3582b6a94e","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-G13","problem_id":"831408a6-08f4-413f-8727-81c32568ae8a","from_version_id":"30f576e4-dc2a-43a6-bb3b-57bcaa63868f","expect":{"options":["reflecting the conviction of","a practice reflecting the conviction of","a practice that reflect the conviction of","reflecting the convictions they held for"],"explanation":"주절 'Students moved fluidly between workshops...'는 이미 완결된 절이며, 그 뒤는 이 전체 행동이 무엇을 '반영하는지' 설명하는 분사구여야 한다. 'reflecting the conviction of'는 현재분사로 앞 절 전체를 의미상 주어로 삼아 자연스럽게 이어지며, 단수 명사 'conviction'이 'its founders'와 결합해 '설립자들의 (하나의) 신념'이라는 논리를 정확히 전달한다. 'a practice reflecting the conviction of'는 동격 명사구를 삽입해 불필요하게 구조를 복잡하게 만들고 콤마 앞 절과의 연결이 어색해진다. 'a practice that reflect the conviction of'는 관계대명사절 동사가 단수 선행사 'a practice'와 수 일치하지 않는 오류(reflect가 아니라 reflects)를 포함한다. 'reflecting the convictions they held for'는 복수 'convictions'를 써서 설립자들이 여러 개별 신념을 가졌다는 잘못된 의미를 만들고, 문장 끝의 'that no single medium...' 절과의 논리적 연결도 어색해진다. 따라서 정답은 A번이다.","explanation_en":"The main clause 'Students moved fluidly between workshops...' is already a complete clause, so what follows must be a participial phrase explaining what this whole behavior 'reflects.' 'Reflecting the conviction of' is a present participle that attaches naturally to the whole preceding clause, and the singular noun 'conviction' combined with 'its founders' conveys the logic of a single shared belief held by the founders. 'A practice reflecting the conviction of' inserts an appositive noun phrase that needlessly complicates the structure and connects awkwardly to the clause before the comma. 'A practice that reflect the conviction of' contains a subject-verb agreement error in the relative clause: the verb should be 'reflects' to agree with the singular antecedent 'a practice.' 'Reflecting the convictions they held for' uses the plural 'convictions,' creating the wrong meaning that the founders held several separate beliefs, and it also connects awkwardly to the 'that no single medium...' clause at the end of the sentence. Therefore, the answer is A."},"set":{"options":["reflecting the conviction of","which reflecting the conviction of","a practice that reflect the conviction of","reflecting the convictions they held for"],"explanation":"주절 'Students moved fluidly between workshops...'는 이미 완결된 절이며, 그 뒤는 이 전체 행동이 무엇을 '반영하는지' 설명하는 분사구여야 한다. 'reflecting the conviction of'는 현재분사로 앞 절 전체를 수식하며 'its founders'와 결합해 '설립자들의 신념'이라는 의미를 정확히 전달한다. 'which reflecting the conviction of'는 관계대명사 뒤에 정동사 없이 분사만 와서 비문이다. 'a practice that reflect the conviction of'는 관계절 동사가 단수 선행사 'a practice'와 수가 맞지 않는다(reflects여야 함). 'reflecting the convictions they held for'는 복수 'convictions'를 써서 설립자들이 여러 개별 신념을 가졌다는 잘못된 의미를 만들고 뒤의 'that no single medium...' 절과도 연결되지 않는다. 따라서 정답은 A이다.","explanation_en":"The main clause \"Students moved fluidly between workshops...\" is already complete, so what follows must be a participial phrase explaining what this whole behavior reflects. \"Reflecting the conviction of\" is a present participle that modifies the whole preceding clause, and combined with \"its founders\" it conveys the founders' single shared belief. \"Which reflecting the conviction of\" places a participle after a relative pronoun with no finite verb, which is ungrammatical. \"A practice that reflect the conviction of\" has a relative-clause verb that does not agree with the singular antecedent \"a practice\" (it should be \"reflects\"). \"Reflecting the convictions they held for\" uses the plural \"convictions,\" suggesting the founders held several separate beliefs, and it does not connect to the \"that no single medium...\" clause. Therefore, the answer is A."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.240Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-G13","fromVersionId":"30f576e4-dc2a-43a6-bb3b-57bcaa63868f","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-M17","problem_id":"0049d229-8bdc-4a0d-a585-c43cbe9e96dd","from_version_id":"e82cad55-81b8-4008-bd8c-762504c08591","expect":{"passage":"The line graph shows the booth's total earnings at 6 different values of the number of tickets a game booth sells; the values follow a linear pattern.","question":"According to the linear relationship shown in the graph, what is the predicted earnings (in dollars) when tickets sold is 5 tickets?","figure":{"categories":["0","1","2","3","4","5"],"kind":"line","series":[{"values":[60,50,40,30,20,10]}],"type":"data","xTitle":"Number of tickets sold","yMax":70,"yMin":0,"yStep":10,"yTitle":"Earnings (dollars)"}},"set":{"passage":"The line graph shows the balance remaining on a prepaid card at 6 different values of the number of games played at a game booth; the values follow a linear pattern.","question":"According to the linear relationship shown in the graph, what is the predicted balance (in dollars) when games played is 5 games?","figure":{"categories":["0","1","2","3","4","5"],"kind":"line","series":[{"values":[60,50,40,30,20,10]}],"type":"data","xTitle":"Number of games played","yMax":70,"yMin":0,"yStep":10,"yTitle":"Balance remaining (dollars)"},"figure_checked":false},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.242Z","issues":[],"alt":"선그래프, 가로축 Number of games played, 세로축 Balance remaining (dollars). 0 60, 1 50, 2 40, 3 30, 4 20, 5 10."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-M17","fromVersionId":"e82cad55-81b8-4008-bd8c-762504c08591","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-M19","problem_id":"359548c6-2d67-4934-92cf-58d5e429bff2","from_version_id":"e8e1cf76-40c5-4201-bac7-85720b4ce53b","expect":{"question":"By how many percent does the phone's battery life remaining change for each increase of 1 hour in the number of hours a phone is used each day?"},"set":{"question":"By how many percentage points does the phone's battery life remaining decrease for each increase of 1 hour in the number of hours a phone is used each day?"},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.245Z","issues":[],"alt":"좌표평면(x 0~8, y 0~60), 가로축 Daily use (hours), 세로축 Battery remaining (percent). 산점도 S1: 점 3개, 추세선 y = -5x + 55."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-M19","fromVersionId":"e8e1cf76-40c5-4201-bac7-85720b4ce53b","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-M20","problem_id":"212ae011-ef9e-4cdc-9cf6-361cf9c8006e","from_version_id":"e8aa64a0-5eb5-4a12-a35c-1ea87e3c8e80","expect":{"question":"How many people should be in the sample for the new survey?"},"set":{"question":"How many households should be in the sample for the new survey?"},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.246Z","issues":[],"alt":"Survey of households in Riverton — 표(2열 2행). 열: Survey detail, Value. Sample size / 200; Margin of error (percentage points) / 12."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-M20","fromVersionId":"e8aa64a0-5eb5-4a12-a35c-1ea87e3c8e80","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-M23","problem_id":"eadbd9ab-c1ce-4f9f-92fd-5d3de35cc2b3","from_version_id":"47cb78b0-9dab-4091-ad19-e3e60f2461a1","expect":{"passage":"An economist writes the total revenue function for a small manufacturing firm as $R(x) = x^3 - 5x^2 - 4x + 20$, where $x$ is the number of units produced (in thousands) and $R(x)$ is revenue (in thousands of dollars). She wants to factor the expression to identify the production levels at which revenue equals zero.\n\nNote: $(x-2)(x+2) = x^2 - 4$, and this expression is not a perfect square trinomial such as $(x-2)^2$ or $(x+2)^2$."},"set":{"passage":"An economist writes the total revenue function for a small manufacturing firm as $R(x) = x^3 - 5x^2 - 4x + 20$, where $x$ is the number of units produced (in thousands) and $R(x)$ is revenue (in thousands of dollars). She wants to factor the expression to identify the production levels at which revenue equals zero."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.250Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-M23","fromVersionId":"47cb78b0-9dab-4091-ad19-e3e60f2461a1","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-M24","problem_id":"8f837ceb-4c51-4a7c-b3ed-2955cdc1f652","from_version_id":"97d1a0ba-ebff-4877-9873-fa4a042041e6","expect":{"passage":"For all real numbers $x$ where the expression is defined, $\\dfrac{x^2 - 5x - 14}{x^2 - 4} = \\dfrac{x - 7}{x - 2}$ is equivalent to which expression?","question":"Which of the following is equivalent to $\\dfrac{x-7}{x-2}$ for all $x$ where the expression is defined?"},"set":{"passage":"The expression $\\dfrac{x-7}{x-2}$ is defined for all real numbers $x$ except $x = 2$.","question":"Which of the following is equivalent to this expression for all $x$ where both expressions are defined?"},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.253Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-M24","fromVersionId":"97d1a0ba-ebff-4877-9873-fa4a042041e6","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-M25","problem_id":"4bcb7479-58a7-4110-8d2d-e2b70e5a8928","from_version_id":"a4cb2c23-91f9-46e3-ab1b-6b476edacb6c","expect":{"passage":"The table shows the price of each of two kinds of cupcakes. A bakery sold 4 plain cupcakes and some frosted cupcakes in one day, spending 32 dollars in all."},"set":{"passage":"The table shows the price of each of two kinds of cupcakes. A customer bought 4 plain cupcakes and some frosted cupcakes from a bakery in one day, spending 32 dollars in all."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.253Z","issues":[],"alt":"표(2열 2행). 열: Cupcake, Price (dollars). Frosted / 6; Plain / 5."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-M25","fromVersionId":"a4cb2c23-91f9-46e3-ab1b-6b476edacb6c","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-P02","problem_id":"b4df4856-bad0-42c1-9d7d-e02f0df9274c","from_version_id":"e1f87f12-ef14-4fb5-8ad4-e235859de4b5","expect":{"options":["Similarly","For example","Eventually","Likewise"],"explanation":"한자 동맹은 오랫동안 중앙 정부 없이 번성했지만, 이후 16세기에 국가 체제가 등장하며 경쟁에서 밀려 해체되었다. 이는 시간의 흐름에 따른 전개를 나타내므로 시간적 전환어인 'Eventually'가 문맥상 논리적으로 맞다. 나머지 선택지는 유사성이나 예시 관계를 나타내어 시간의 경과에 따른 결과를 표현하지 못한다.","explanation_en":"The Hanseatic League thrived for a long time without a central government, but later, in the 16th century, nation-states emerged, and it was pushed out of the competition and dissolved. This shows development over time, so the temporal transition 'Eventually' fits the context logically. The other choices indicate a relationship of similarity or example and cannot express a result that comes with the passage of time."},"set":{"options":["Eventually;","Eventually:","Eventually","Eventually and"],"explanation":"빈칸 뒤에는 \", when nation-states began ...\"라는 부사절과 완전한 주절이 이어지므로 빈칸에는 문장 앞에 놓이는 부사 하나만 들어가야 한다. \"Eventually\"가 이 조건을 만족한다. \"Eventually;\"와 \"Eventually:\"는 이어지는 쉼표와 겹쳐 문장부호가 이중이 되고 문장 경계도 잘못 만들며, \"Eventually and\"는 연결할 대상이 없는 접속사가 남는다. 따라서 정답은 C이다.","explanation_en":"After the blank come \", when nation-states began ...\" and a complete main clause, so the blank must hold a single adverb that opens the sentence. \"Eventually\" meets that condition. \"Eventually;\" and \"Eventually:\" collide with the comma that follows, creating double punctuation and a faulty sentence boundary, and \"Eventually and\" leaves a conjunction with nothing to join. So the answer is C."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.253Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-P02","fromVersionId":"e1f87f12-ef14-4fb5-8ad4-e235859de4b5","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-P03","problem_id":"4c9ee2b7-687b-43c6-a04d-52448f943950","from_version_id":"079eeaee-2165-4f48-a1f7-86e82cfce1b9","expect":{"passage":"In 1959, engineers at the Dutch firm Philips faced a puzzle: their new compact cassette needed to be small enough for a coat pocket yet durable enough to survive years of handling. Lou Ottens, the project's lead ______ this constraint by first carving a prototype from a block of wood that fit inside his jacket, then insisting that every later design match its dimensions."},"set":{"passage":"In the early 1960s, engineers at the Dutch firm Philips faced a puzzle: their new compact cassette needed to be small enough for a coat pocket yet durable enough to survive years of handling. Lou Ottens, the project's lead ______ this constraint by first carving a prototype from a block of wood that fit inside his jacket, then insisting that every later design match its dimensions."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.254Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-P03","fromVersionId":"079eeaee-2165-4f48-a1f7-86e82cfce1b9","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-P04","problem_id":"0cdf0650-bc5c-4dc5-ab5a-2efa21e968a2","from_version_id":"3b7d7eba-df78-4de2-b253-5354517cf451","expect":{"passage":"In the early stages of designing a new suspension bridge, engineers must account for the fact that wind can cause a structure to oscillate at increasingly larger amplitudes if the frequency of the gusts matches the bridge's natural frequency. Historical failures, most notably the collapse of the Tacoma Narrows Bridge in 1940, demonstrated how quickly this effect can become destructive. Modern designers therefore run extensive wind-tunnel simulations to ______ potential resonance problems long before construction begins, adjusting the deck shape and cable tension until the models show stable behavior under a wide range of wind conditions.","explanation":"지문은 엔지니어들이 역사적 붕괴 사례(타코마 다리)를 근거로, 건설 전에 풍동 실험을 통해 공진 문제를 미리 파악하고 설계를 조정한다고 설명한다. 따라서 빈칸에는 문제를 사전에 '예측하다(anticipate)'가 가장 적절하다. overlook(간과하다)은 문제를 미리 파악한다는 문맥과 정반대의 태도를 나타낸다. celebrate(기념하다)는 공진 문제와 전혀 무관한 의미이다. tolerate(참아내다)는 문제를 없애기 위해 설계를 조정한다는 목적과는 관련이 있어 보이지만, '미리 발견하여 대비한다'는 핵심 의미를 담지 못하므로 적절하지 않다.","explanation_en":"The passage explains that, based on historical failures (the Tacoma bridge), engineers identify resonance problems in advance through wind-tunnel tests before construction and adjust their designs. Therefore, \"anticipate\" is the best fit for the blank. \"Overlook\" describes an attitude directly opposite to identifying problems in advance. \"Celebrate\" has nothing to do with resonance problems. \"Tolerate\" might seem related because designs are adjusted to eliminate problems, but it does not capture the key idea of discovering problems early and preparing for them, so it is not appropriate."},"set":{"passage":"In the early stages of designing a new suspension bridge, engineers must account for the fact that wind can cause a structure to oscillate at increasingly larger amplitudes if the forces exerted by the wind keep feeding energy into the motion. Historical failures, most notably the collapse of the Tacoma Narrows Bridge in 1940, demonstrated how quickly such oscillation can become destructive. Modern designers therefore run extensive wind-tunnel simulations to ______ potential oscillation problems long before construction begins, adjusting the deck shape and cable tension until the models show stable behavior under a wide range of wind conditions.","explanation":"지문은 엔지니어들이 역사적 붕괴 사례(타코마 다리)를 근거로, 건설 전에 풍동 실험을 통해 진동 문제를 미리 파악하고 설계를 조정한다고 설명한다. 따라서 빈칸에는 문제를 사전에 '예측하다(anticipate)'가 가장 적절하다. overlook(간과하다)은 문제를 미리 파악한다는 문맥과 정반대의 태도를 나타낸다. celebrate(기념하다)는 진동 문제와 전혀 무관한 의미이다. tolerate(참아내다)는 문제를 없애기 위해 설계를 조정한다는 목적과는 관련이 있어 보이지만, '미리 발견하여 대비한다'는 핵심 의미를 담지 못하므로 적절하지 않다.","explanation_en":"The passage explains that, based on historical failures (the Tacoma bridge), engineers identify oscillation problems in advance through wind-tunnel tests before construction and adjust their designs. Therefore, \"anticipate\" is the best fit for the blank. \"Overlook\" describes an attitude directly opposite to identifying problems in advance. \"Celebrate\" has nothing to do with oscillation problems. \"Tolerate\" might seem related because designs are adjusted to eliminate problems, but it does not capture the key idea of discovering problems early and preparing for them, so it is not appropriate."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.254Z","issues":[],"alt":"Suspension Bridge Wind-Tunnel Testing — 자료: Historical event Tacoma Narrows Bridge collapse 1940, Testing method Wind-tunnel simulations, Design goal Stable behavior under a wide range of wind conditions. Engineers adjust deck shape and cable tension until models show stable behavior.."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-P04","fromVersionId":"3b7d7eba-df78-4de2-b253-5354517cf451","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-P05-sponge","problem_id":"59115b48-1695-4f87-a971-4b741373d0ec","from_version_id":"4d4ebba9-8800-4be8-aff6-16cd3b2440c9","expect":{"options":["Deep-sea sponges on the Arctic Mid-Ocean Ridge recycle nutrients from dead sponge skeletons in a way that supports diverse surrounding life, overturning the earlier view that they were merely passive feeders.","Microbes, worms, and crustaceans depend entirely on deep-sea sponges for their survival because no other nutrient sources exist on the abyssal plain.","Sponge gardens on the Arctic Mid-Ocean Ridge are primarily notable because they convert silica into new mineral deposits that other organisms then colonize.","Because the sponges break down silica skeletons into usable nutrients, the surrounding microbes, worms, and crustaceans no longer need any other source of food to survive on the abyssal plain."],"passage":"For decades, marine biologists assumed that deep-sea sponges were passive filter feeders with little influence on their surroundings. Recent surveys of the Arctic Mid-Ocean Ridge, however, have revealed vast \"sponge gardens\" that appear to reshape the seafloor ecosystem they inhabit. These sponges consume the silica skeletons of long-dead relatives, converting an otherwise inert mineral deposit into a source of nutrients for themselves and for the microbes, worms, and crustaceans that cluster around their bases. Because the sponges recycle nutrients that would otherwise remain locked in the seabed, entire communities of organisms that could not survive on the nutrient-poor abyssal plain are able to flourish nearby. Researchers now regard these sponge grounds not as isolated curiosities but as keystone habitats that sustain biodiversity across otherwise barren stretches of ocean floor.","explanation":"지문은 심해 해면이 오랫동안 수동적 여과 섭식자로만 여겨졌다는 통념을 제시한 뒤, 최근 조사 결과 해면이 죽은 동족의 규산질 골격을 분해해 영양분으로 전환하고 이를 주변 생물군에게 공급함으로써 생태계 전체를 지탱하는 핵심종 역할을 한다는 새로운 발견을 전달한다. 따라서 중심 생각은 해면의 영양분 재활용이 주변 생물다양성을 뒷받침하며 기존의 수동적 존재라는 인식을 뒤집는다는 것이다. 이는 선택지 0에 정확히 요약되어 있다. 나머지 선택지는 지문의 일부 세부사항(전면적 의존, 오래된 통념, 유일한 광물 퇴적물)만 반영하거나 지문과 모순되어 중심 생각을 대표하지 못한다.","explanation_en":"The text presents the long-held belief that deep-sea sponges are merely passive filter feeders, then conveys a new finding from a recent survey: sponges break down the silica skeletons of their dead relatives, convert them into nutrients, and supply these to the surrounding community, acting as a keystone species that supports the whole ecosystem. So the main idea is that the sponges' nutrient recycling supports the surrounding biodiversity and overturns the view of them as passive. This is summarized exactly in the first choice (A). The other choices reflect only some details of the text (total dependence, the old belief, the only mineral deposits) or contradict it, so they do not represent the main idea."},"set":{"options":["Deep-sea sponges on the Arctic Mid-Ocean Ridge recycle dissolved nutrients in a way that supports diverse surrounding life, overturning the earlier view that they were merely passive feeders.","Microbes, worms, and crustaceans depend entirely on deep-sea sponges for their survival because no other nutrient sources exist on the abyssal plain.","Sponge gardens on the Arctic Mid-Ocean Ridge are primarily notable because they convert dissolved organic matter into new mineral deposits that other organisms then colonize.","Because the sponges turn dissolved organic matter into usable detritus, the surrounding microbes, worms, and crustaceans no longer need any other source of food to survive on the abyssal plain."],"passage":"For decades, marine biologists assumed that deep-sea sponges were passive filter feeders with little influence on their surroundings. Recent surveys of the Arctic Mid-Ocean Ridge, however, have revealed vast \"sponge gardens\" that appear to reshape the seafloor ecosystem they inhabit. These sponges take up dissolved organic matter from the surrounding water and release it as particles of detritus, converting a nutrient source that would otherwise remain out of reach into food for the microbes, worms, and crustaceans that cluster around their bases. Because the sponges recycle nutrients that would otherwise stay dispersed in the water, entire communities of organisms that could not survive on the nutrient-poor abyssal plain are able to flourish nearby. Researchers now regard these sponge grounds not as isolated curiosities but as keystone habitats that sustain biodiversity across otherwise barren stretches of ocean floor.","explanation":"지문은 심해 해면이 오랫동안 수동적 여과 섭식자로만 여겨졌다는 통념을 제시한 뒤, 최근 조사 결과 해면이 주변 물속의 용존 유기물을 흡수해 입자성 부스러기로 내놓음으로써 주변 생물군에 먹이를 공급하고 생태계 전체를 지탱하는 핵심종 역할을 한다는 새로운 발견을 전달한다. 따라서 중심 생각은 해면의 영양분 재활용이 주변 생물다양성을 뒷받침하며 기존의 수동적 존재라는 인식을 뒤집는다는 것이다. 이는 A에 정확히 요약되어 있다. 나머지 선택지는 지문의 일부 세부사항(전면적 의존, 새로운 광물 퇴적물)만 반영하거나 지문과 모순되어 중심 생각을 대표하지 못한다.","explanation_en":"The text presents the long-held belief that deep-sea sponges are merely passive filter feeders, then reports a recent finding: sponges take up dissolved organic matter from the surrounding water and release it as particles of detritus, feeding the surrounding community and acting as a keystone habitat that supports the whole ecosystem. The main idea is therefore that the sponges' nutrient recycling supports the surrounding biodiversity and overturns the view of them as passive. This is summarized exactly in A. The other choices reflect only some details of the text (total dependence, new mineral deposits) or contradict it, so they do not state the main idea."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.254Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-P05-sponge","fromVersionId":"4d4ebba9-8800-4be8-aff6-16cd3b2440c9","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-P05-bridge","problem_id":"fa015adb-185f-4b33-ae6a-5de398a1143e","from_version_id":"7beed615-1732-4398-9889-84c87214922e","expect":{"passage":"In 2019, engineers in the Netherlands opened a small bicycle bridge built almost entirely from recycled plastic bottles and packaging. The bridge's hollow sections are much lighter than concrete ______ they can be shipped to a site and installed in a single day.","explanation":"빈칸 앞 \"The bridge's hollow sections are much lighter than concrete slabs\"와 뒤 \"they can be shipped ... in a single day\"는 각각 완전한 독립절이다. 두 독립절을 잇려면 쉼표와 등위접속사가 함께 필요하므로 D가 정답이다. A는 쉼표만으로 두 독립절을 이어 쉼표 스플라이스가 된다. B는 아무 문장부호나 접속사 없이 이어 융합문(run-on)이 된다. C는 접속사 \"and\" 뒤에 불필요한 쉼표를 넣어 \"and\"와 절 사이를 부적절하게 끊는다.","explanation_en":"The words before the blank, \"The bridge's hollow sections are much lighter than concrete slabs,\" and the words after it, \"they can be shipped ... in a single day,\" are each independent clauses. To join two independent clauses, you need a comma plus a coordinating conjunction, so D is correct. A joins them with only a comma, creating a comma splice. B uses no punctuation or conjunction at all, creating a run-on sentence. C adds an unnecessary comma after \"and,\" which wrongly separates the conjunction from the clause it introduces."},"set":{"passage":"In 2018, engineers in the Netherlands opened a short bicycle path built from hollow modules made of recycled plastic. The path's hollow sections are much lighter than concrete ______ they can be shipped to a site and installed quickly.","explanation":"빈칸 앞 \"The path's hollow sections are much lighter than concrete slabs\"와 뒤 \"they can be shipped ... installed quickly\"는 각각 완전한 독립절이다. 두 독립절을 잇려면 쉼표와 등위접속사가 함께 필요하므로 D가 정답이다. A는 쉼표만으로 두 독립절을 이어 쉼표 스플라이스가 된다. B는 아무 문장부호나 접속사 없이 이어 융합문(run-on)이 된다. C는 접속사 \"and\" 뒤에 불필요한 쉼표를 넣어 \"and\"와 절 사이를 부적절하게 끊는다.","explanation_en":"The words before the blank, \"The path's hollow sections are much lighter than concrete slabs,\" and the words after it, \"they can be shipped ... installed quickly,\" are each independent clauses. To join two independent clauses, you need a comma plus a coordinating conjunction, so D is correct. A joins them with only a comma, creating a comma splice. B uses no punctuation or conjunction at all, creating a run-on sentence. C adds an unnecessary comma after \"and,\" which wrongly separates the conjunction from the clause it introduces."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.254Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-P05-bridge","fromVersionId":"7beed615-1732-4398-9889-84c87214922e","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-P05-fern","problem_id":"40c78fe3-ff66-4f3e-b4eb-12837f9ef8e2","from_version_id":"0e7c60a5-cddf-4bb8-8177-c4a2b161dd29","expect":{"passage":"Most desert plants survive dry seasons by storing water in thick stems or leaves. The resurrection fern takes a very different approach. During a drought, its fronds curl up, turn brown, and lose nearly all of their water, so the plant looks completely dead. ______ within a day of a heavy rain, the same fronds absorb water, uncurl, and turn bright green again."},"set":{"passage":"Many plants that endure dry seasons, such as desert succulents, survive by storing water in thick stems or leaves. The resurrection fern takes a very different approach. During a drought, its fronds curl up, turn brown, and lose nearly all of their water, so the plant looks completely dead. ______ within a day of a heavy rain, the same fronds absorb water, uncurl, and turn bright green again."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.254Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-P05-fern","fromVersionId":"0e7c60a5-cddf-4bb8-8177-c4a2b161dd29","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-P05-frb","problem_id":"52cd5e73-5a6b-4779-ae38-d81ecc8df996","from_version_id":"f149a4e5-a70f-48a1-a34a-a3341997f01f","expect":{"passage":"When astronomers first detected fast radio bursts, brief and extraordinarily powerful flashes of radio waves originating from beyond our galaxy, most of the recorded events were one-time occurrences that never repeated, making it nearly impossible to trace their sources or determine their causes. In 2016, however, researchers identified a burst source in a dwarf galaxy that repeated dozens of times over several months, allowing astronomers to pinpoint its location with far greater precision than any single burst had permitted. __This repeating source let scientists train radio telescopes on the same patch of sky in anticipation of future bursts, eventually enabling them to associate the phenomenon with a specific type of highly magnetized neutron star.__ Without a repeating signal to study, researchers would have continued relying on brief, unpredictable detections that offered little opportunity for sustained observation."},"set":{"passage":"When astronomers first detected fast radio bursts, brief and extraordinarily powerful flashes of radio waves originating from beyond our galaxy, most of the recorded events were one-time occurrences that never repeated, making it nearly impossible to trace their sources or determine their causes. In 2016, however, researchers identified a burst source in a dwarf galaxy that repeated multiple times, allowing astronomers to pinpoint its location with far greater precision than any single burst had permitted. __This repeating source let scientists train radio telescopes on the same patch of sky in anticipation of future bursts, eventually enabling them to associate the phenomenon with a specific type of highly magnetized neutron star.__ Without a repeating signal to study, researchers would have continued relying on brief, unpredictable detections that offered little opportunity for sustained observation."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.254Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-P05-frb","fromVersionId":"f149a4e5-a70f-48a1-a34a-a3341997f01f","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-P05-fern2","problem_id":"8614efec-7183-4191-af98-39f618153e03","from_version_id":"da7d7e66-82a8-45e8-8951-2a9566517e30","expect":{"options":["To argue that resurrection ferns are more resilient than most other desert organisms","To explain the biological mechanism that allows the plants to survive the extreme water loss described earlier","To describe the negative long-term consequences that severe dehydration has on plant tissue","To conclude the passage by summarizing the plant's complete life cycle from birth to death"],"passage":"Certain desert plants, such as the resurrection fern, have evolved the remarkable ability to survive prolonged periods of extreme dehydration by essentially shutting down their metabolic processes. During dry spells, these plants can lose up to ninety-five percent of their water content, curling their leaves inward and appearing completely dead. __Rather than dying, however, the plants enter a dormant state in which cellular damage is minimized through the production of specialized sugars that stabilize proteins and membranes.__ When rain finally returns, the plants rehydrate within hours, unfurling their leaves and resuming normal photosynthesis as though the dry period had never occurred.","explanation":"밑줄 문장은 'Rather than dying, however'라는 표현으로 앞 문장의 죽은 것처럼 보이는 상태를 재해석하며, 특수 당분 생성을 통한 세포 손상 최소화라는 구체적 생존 메커니즘을 설명하는 핵심 주장 역할을 한다. B는 이를 정확히 서술한다. A는 다른 사막 생물과의 비교를 잘못 상정하며(지문에 없음), C는 지문이 부정적 장기 영향이 아니라 생존 메커니즘을 설명하므로 틀리고, D는 지문 전체가 생애주기 요약이 아니라 탈수 생존 메커니즘에 집중하므로 범위를 벗어난다.","explanation_en":"The underlined sentence begins with 'Rather than dying, however,' reinterprets the seemingly dead state described in the previous sentence, and acts as the key claim explaining a specific survival mechanism, in which special sugars minimize cell damage. B describes this accurately. A wrongly assumes a comparison with other desert organisms (not in the text). C is wrong because the text explains a survival mechanism, not negative long-term effects. D goes beyond the scope because the whole text focuses on the mechanism of surviving dehydration, not a summary of the life cycle."},"set":{"options":["To argue that resurrection ferns are more resilient than most other plants","To explain the biological mechanism that allows the plants to survive the extreme water loss described earlier","To describe the negative long-term consequences that severe dehydration has on plant tissue","To conclude the passage by summarizing the plant's complete life cycle from birth to death"],"passage":"Certain plants, such as the resurrection fern, have evolved the remarkable ability to survive prolonged periods of extreme dehydration by essentially shutting down their metabolic processes. During dry spells, these plants can lose up to ninety-five percent of their water content, curling their leaves inward and appearing completely dead. __Rather than dying, however, the plants enter a dormant state in which cellular damage is minimized through the production of specialized sugars that stabilize proteins and membranes.__ When rain finally returns, the plants rehydrate within hours, unfurling their leaves and resuming normal photosynthesis as though the dry period had never occurred.","explanation":"밑줄 문장은 'Rather than dying, however'라는 표현으로 앞 문장의 죽은 것처럼 보이는 상태를 재해석하며, 특수 당분 생성을 통한 세포 손상 최소화라는 구체적 생존 메커니즘을 설명하는 핵심 주장 역할을 한다. B는 이를 정확히 서술한다. A는 다른 식물과의 비교를 잘못 상정하며(지문에 없음), C는 지문이 부정적 장기 영향이 아니라 생존 메커니즘을 설명하므로 틀리고, D는 지문 전체가 생애주기 요약이 아니라 탈수 생존 메커니즘에 집중하므로 범위를 벗어난다.","explanation_en":"The underlined sentence begins with 'Rather than dying, however,' reinterprets the seemingly dead state described in the previous sentence, and acts as the key claim explaining a specific survival mechanism, in which special sugars minimize cell damage. B describes this accurately. A wrongly assumes a comparison with other plants (not in the text). C is wrong because the text explains a survival mechanism, not negative long-term effects. D goes beyond the scope because the whole text focuses on the mechanism of surviving dehydration, not a summary of the life cycle."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.254Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-P05-fern2","fromVersionId":"da7d7e66-82a8-45e8-8951-2a9566517e30","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-P05-ngc","problem_id":"9543e92d-f90c-4314-bfa1-50d02d356866","from_version_id":"7bdf6e57-d9c3-4ea8-910e-cf0531cfc409","expect":{"options":["All stars in NGC 6791 are now known to have formed at slightly different times from separate collapsing gas clouds.","Stellar mergers in NGC 6791 made some stars appear older than they actually were, revealing a limitation in age estimates that rely only on brightness and chemical composition.","Spectroscopic analysis showed that the merged stars in NGC 6791 were in fact older than the rest of the cluster.","Astronomers concluded that brightness alone is always a reliable method for estimating the age of any star in a cluster."],"passage":"Astronomers studying the star cluster NGC 6791 were puzzled by an apparent contradiction: some stars in the cluster appeared far older than others, even though all stars in a single cluster are thought to form from the same collapsing gas cloud at roughly the same time. Detailed spectroscopic analysis eventually revealed the likely explanation. A subset of the cluster's stars had, at some point, merged with a nearby stellar companion, and this merger process altered their surface chemistry and apparent brightness in ways that mimicked the signatures normally associated with advanced stellar age. Once astronomers accounted for these mergers, the estimated ages of the affected stars aligned much more closely with the rest of the cluster. The finding suggests that age estimates based on brightness and chemical composition alone can be misleading for any star that has undergone a stellar merger.","explanation":"지문은 NGC 6791 성단에서 일부 별들의 겉보기 나이가 실제보다 많아 보였던 원인이 항성 병합(stellar merger)에 있음을 설명한다. 근거 문장에 따르면, 병합 과정이 별의 표면 화학 조성과 밝기를 변화시켜 마치 나이가 많은 별에서 나타나는 신호처럼 보이게 만들었다. 이후 병합 사실을 감안하자 나이 추정치가 성단의 다른 별들과 일치했다는 점에서, 이 발견의 핵심은 '병합이 나이 신호를 왜곡시켰고, 이는 밝기·화학 조성만으로 나이를 추정하는 방법의 한계를 보여준다'는 것이다. 따라서 정답은 B이다. A는 지문이 명시한 전제(모든 별이 같은 가스 구름에서 거의 동시에 형성됨)와 정면으로 모순되어 지문에서 뒷받침되지 않는다(UNSUPPORTED_BY_TEXT). C는 병합된 별들이 '실제로' 더 나이가 많다고 진술하여 병합이 나이를 왜곡했다는 핵심 관계 대신 세부 사실을 반대로 왜곡한 것으로, 중심 생각이 아닌 잘못된 초점에 해당한다(WRONG_FOCUS). D는 지문이 말한 '병합된 별에 한해 한계가 있다'는 제한된 결론을 '항상 신뢰할 수 있다'는 식으로 과도하게 일반화하여 지문의 결론과 반대된다(OVERGENERALIZED).","explanation_en":"The passage explains that some stars in the NGC 6791 cluster looked older than they really were because of stellar mergers. According to the evidence sentences, the merger process changed the stars' surface chemical composition and brightness, so they showed signals that resembled those of older stars. Once the mergers were taken into account, the age estimates matched those of the other stars in the cluster, so the key point of the finding is that mergers distorted the age signals, revealing a limitation of estimating age from brightness and chemical composition alone. So the answer is B. A directly contradicts the premise the passage states (that all the stars formed at nearly the same time from the same gas cloud), so the passage does not support it (UNSUPPORTED_BY_TEXT). C says the merged stars are 'actually' older, reversing a detail instead of stating the key relationship that mergers distorted the age, which is the wrong focus (WRONG_FOCUS). D overgeneralizes the passage's limited conclusion (that the method has limits for merged stars) into the claim that brightness is 'always' reliable, which is the opposite of the passage's conclusion (OVERGENERALIZED)."},"set":{"options":["All stars in NGC 6791 are now known to have formed at slightly different times from separate collapsing gas clouds.","Stellar mergers in NGC 6791 made some stars appear younger than they actually were, revealing a limitation in age estimates that rely only on brightness and chemical composition.","Spectroscopic analysis showed that the merged stars in NGC 6791 were in fact younger than the rest of the cluster.","Astronomers concluded that brightness alone is always a reliable method for estimating the age of any star in a cluster."],"passage":"Astronomers studying the star cluster NGC 6791 were puzzled by an apparent contradiction: some stars in the cluster appeared far younger than others, even though all stars in a single cluster are thought to form from the same collapsing gas cloud at roughly the same time. Detailed spectroscopic analysis eventually revealed the likely explanation. A subset of the cluster's stars had, at some point, merged with a nearby stellar companion, and this merger process altered their surface chemistry and apparent brightness in ways that mimicked the signatures normally associated with youth. Once astronomers accounted for these mergers, the estimated ages of the affected stars aligned much more closely with the rest of the cluster. The finding suggests that age estimates based on brightness and chemical composition alone can be misleading for any star that has undergone a stellar merger.","explanation":"지문은 NGC 6791 성단에서 일부 별들의 겉보기 나이가 실제보다 젊어 보였던 원인이 항성 병합(stellar merger)에 있음을 설명한다. 근거 문장에 따르면, 병합 과정이 별의 표면 화학 조성과 밝기를 변화시켜 마치 젊은 별에서 나타나는 신호처럼 보이게 만들었다. 이후 병합 사실을 감안하자 나이 추정치가 성단의 다른 별들과 일치했다는 점에서, 이 발견의 핵심은 '병합이 나이 신호를 왜곡시켰고, 이는 밝기·화학 조성만으로 나이를 추정하는 방법의 한계를 보여준다'는 것이다. 따라서 정답은 B이다. A는 지문이 명시한 전제(모든 별이 같은 가스 구름에서 거의 동시에 형성됨)와 정면으로 모순되어 지문에서 뒷받침되지 않는다. C는 병합된 별들이 '실제로' 더 젊다고 진술하여 병합이 나이 신호를 왜곡했다는 핵심 관계 대신 세부 사실을 왜곡한 것으로, 잘못된 초점이다. D는 지문이 말한 '병합된 별에 한해 한계가 있다'는 제한된 결론을 '항상 신뢰할 수 있다'는 식으로 과도하게 일반화하여 지문의 결론과 반대된다.","explanation_en":"The passage explains that some stars in the NGC 6791 cluster looked younger than they really were because of stellar mergers. According to the evidence sentences, the merger process changed the stars' surface chemical composition and brightness, so they showed signals that resembled those of young stars. Once the mergers were taken into account, the age estimates matched those of the other stars in the cluster, so the key point of the finding is that mergers distorted the age signals, revealing a limitation of estimating age from brightness and chemical composition alone. So the answer is B. A directly contradicts the premise the passage states (that all the stars formed at nearly the same time from the same gas cloud), so the passage does not support it. C says the merged stars are 'actually' younger, distorting a detail instead of stating the key relationship that mergers distorted the age signal, which is the wrong focus. D overgeneralizes the passage's limited conclusion (that the method has limits for merged stars) into the claim that brightness is 'always' reliable, which is the opposite of the passage's conclusion."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.254Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-P05-ngc","fromVersionId":"7bdf6e57-d9c3-4ea8-910e-cf0531cfc409","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-SCAN-318348e4","problem_id":"318348e4-6f29-4a78-92ec-2e2d427f5c54","from_version_id":"63471e06-d467-43e4-9532-f1ce09223466","expect":{"options":["ended, the","ended; the","ended the, fixed exchange rate era","ended the"],"explanation":"동사 ended는 목적어 the fixed exchange rate era를 바로 취해야 하는 타동사 구조이므로 동사와 목적어 사이에 어떤 구두점도 들어갈 수 없다. 콤마나 콜론을 삽입하면 동사-목적어 관계를 부당하게 끊어 문장 경계 오류가 발생한다. 따라서 정답은 D번 \"ended the\"이다.","explanation_en":"The verb ended is a transitive verb that must take its object, the fixed exchange rate era, directly, so no punctuation can come between the verb and its object. Inserting a comma or a colon would unjustifiably separate the verb from its object and create a sentence-boundary error. Therefore, the answer is D, \"ended the.\""},"set":{"options":["ended,","ended;","ended:","ended"],"explanation":"동사 ended는 뒤에 오는 목적어 the fixed exchange rate era를 바로 취하는 타동사이므로 동사와 목적어 사이에 문장부호가 들어갈 수 없다. 콤마, 세미콜론, 콜론은 모두 동사와 목적어를 부당하게 끊는다. 따라서 문장부호 없이 동사만 쓴 \"ended\"가 정답이며, 정답은 D이다.","explanation_en":"The verb ended is a transitive verb that takes its object, the fixed exchange rate era, directly, so no punctuation can come between the verb and its object. A comma, a semicolon, or a colon would each separate the verb from its object unjustifiably. The correct choice is therefore \"ended\" with no punctuation, so the answer is D."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.254Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-SCAN-318348e4","fromVersionId":"63471e06-d467-43e4-9532-f1ce09223466","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-SCAN-53e14b14","problem_id":"53e14b14-84c1-4c97-8093-36357462314a","from_version_id":"4375e767-bccb-4b35-a65f-2aa546f0ffd4","expect":{"passage":"The Dutch painter Judith Leyster was largely forgotten for more than two centuries after her death, and many of her paintings were misattributed to her contemporary Frans Hals. ______, art historians in the 1890s discovered her distinctive monogram hidden beneath layers of overpainting on several canvases, which prompted a reassessment of her body of work. Today, Leyster is recognized as one of the few women admitted to the Haarlem painters' guild during the seventeenth century."},"set":{"passage":"The Dutch painter Judith Leyster was largely forgotten for more than two centuries after her death, and many of her paintings were misattributed to her contemporary Frans Hals. ______ art historians in the 1890s discovered her distinctive monogram hidden beneath layers of overpainting on several canvases, which prompted a reassessment of her body of work. Today, Leyster is recognized as one of the few women admitted to the Haarlem painters' guild during the seventeenth century."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.254Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-SCAN-53e14b14","fromVersionId":"4375e767-bccb-4b35-a65f-2aa546f0ffd4","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-F-c5eb51a6","problem_id":"c5eb51a6-435a-4508-8203-adbcc03f7c80","from_version_id":"eeb73f45-5840-4902-b335-ec31e3205d9c","expect":{"figure":{"axes":{"x":{"max":10,"min":-10,"step":2,"title":"x"},"y":{"max":10,"min":-10,"step":2,"title":"y"}},"objects":[{"id":"A1","kind":"line","label":"f","through":[[-8,-2],[-2,4]]},{"id":"B1","kind":"line","label":"g","through":[[4,3],[6,7]]}],"type":"plane"}},"set":{"figure":{"axes":{"x":{"max":20,"min":-20,"step":4,"title":"x"},"y":{"max":20,"min":-20,"step":4,"title":"y"}},"objects":[{"id":"A1","kind":"line","label":"f","through":[[-8,-2],[-2,4]]},{"id":"B1","kind":"line","label":"g","through":[[4,3],[6,7]]}],"type":"plane"},"figure_checked":false},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.256Z","issues":[],"alt":"좌표평면(x -20~20, y -20~20). 직선 f: y = x + 6; 직선 g: y = 2x − 5."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-F-c5eb51a6","fromVersionId":"eeb73f45-5840-4902-b335-ec31e3205d9c","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-N01","problem_id":"a422e2b1-ade3-48b9-90d3-7013a98f6803","from_version_id":"7a7e3c95-775c-4c19-812e-0a4b79021d82","expect":{"options":["that,","which","that","that:"],"explanation":"빈칸 뒤의 \"occurs when the plankton are disturbed by movement in the water\"는 앞의 명사 \"a chemical reaction\"을 수식하는 관계절이다. 관계대명사 that은 선행사와 관계절 사이에 어떠한 구두점도 없이 곧바로 연결되어야 한다. 쉼표, 세미콜론, 콜론은 모두 관계절을 문장의 나머지 부분과 분리시켜 구조를 깨뜨리므로 오답이다. 정답은 C이다.","explanation_en":"The clause 'occurs when the plankton are disturbed by movement in the water' after the blank is a relative clause modifying the preceding noun 'a chemical reaction.' The relative pronoun that must connect directly to its antecedent and the relative clause with no punctuation in between. The comma, semicolon, and colon all separate the relative clause from the rest of the sentence and break the structure, so they are wrong. The answer is C."},"set":{"options":["that,","who","that","that:"],"explanation":"빈칸 뒤의 \"occurs when the plankton are disturbed by movement in the water\"는 앞의 명사 \"a chemical reaction\"을 수식하는 관계절이다. 관계대명사 that은 선행사와 관계절 사이에 어떠한 구두점도 없이 곧바로 연결되어야 한다. 쉼표, 세미콜론, 콜론은 모두 관계절을 문장의 나머지 부분과 분리시켜 구조를 깨뜨리므로 오답이다. 또한 \"who\"는 사람을 가리키는 관계대명사라 사물인 \"a chemical reaction\"을 수식할 수 없다. 정답은 C이다.","explanation_en":"The clause 'occurs when the plankton are disturbed by movement in the water' after the blank is a relative clause modifying the preceding noun 'a chemical reaction.' The relative pronoun that must connect directly to its antecedent and the relative clause with no punctuation in between. The comma, semicolon, and colon all separate the relative clause from the rest of the sentence and break the structure, so they are wrong. \"Who\" is a relative pronoun for people and cannot modify \"a chemical reaction.\" The answer is C."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.256Z","issues":[]},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-N01","fromVersionId":"7a7e3c95-775c-4c19-812e-0a4b79021d82","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-N02","problem_id":"abc08190-cb4e-4189-a3e6-325fb165a973","from_version_id":"4c44aa85-359d-45c1-8b39-f760da7a7eac","expect":{"passage":"A marine biologist studied the average depth at which five species of sea turtles were observed foraging along a coastline. The table below shows the average foraging depth, in meters, recorded for each species.\n\nSea Turtle Species | Average Foraging Depth (meters)\nLoggerhead | 22\nLeatherback | 19\nGreen | 8\nHawksbill | 6\nOlive Ridley | 5\n\nThe biologist claims that among the sea turtle species studied, the loggerhead turtle was observed foraging at the greatest average depth."},"set":{"passage":"A marine biologist studied the average depth at which five species of sea turtles were observed foraging along a coastline. The table below shows the average foraging depth, in meters, recorded for each species.\n\nThe biologist claims that among the sea turtle species studied, the loggerhead turtle was observed foraging at the greatest average depth."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.256Z","issues":[],"alt":"Average Foraging Depth by Sea Turtle Species — 표(2열 5행). 열: Sea Turtle Species, Average Foraging Depth (meters). Loggerhead / 22; Leatherback / 19; Green / 8; Hawksbill / 6; Olive Ridley / 5."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-N02","fromVersionId":"4c44aa85-359d-45c1-8b39-f760da7a7eac","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-T1","problem_id":"00b041df-ea9a-4a4b-a1a3-8fccd7fbc39f","from_version_id":"0d91f902-bd4f-43e4-91fd-5c8f27e6fdbf","expect":{"explanation":"(1) 각 행에서 6x + 5y 를 계산한다. (2) 그 값이 27 보다 작다고 할 수 있는지 확인한다. (3) 경계(같은 경우)의 포함 여부도 확인한다. (4) 정답 표의 각 행 (1, 4), (2, 1), (3, 0) 은 모두 조건을 만족한다. (5) 다른 표는 부등호 방향·경계·한 행·x 와 y 의 순서 중 하나가 어긋난다. 따라서 정답은 D이다.","explanation_en":"(1) Compute the left side. (2) Compare with the constant. (3) Check the boundary rule. (4) Every row of the correct table satisfies the condition. (5) Each other table fails in exactly one way. So the answer is D."},"set":{"explanation":"표가 정답이려면 모든 행이 부등식 $6c + 5d < 27$ 를 만족해야 한다. 즉 좌변의 값이 27보다 작아야 한다. 각 행을 대입하면 다음과 같다. 표 A: $(1, 12)$ 에서 $6 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 12 = 66$ (불성립); $(2, 7)$ 에서 $6 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 7 = 47$ (불성립); $(3, 8)$ 에서 $6 \\cdot 3 + 5 \\cdot 8 = 58$ (불성립). 표 B: $(1, 4)$ 에서 $6 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 4 = 26$ (성립); $(2, 3)$ 에서 $6 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 3 = 27$ (불성립); $(3, 0)$ 에서 $6 \\cdot 3 + 5 \\cdot 0 = 18$ (성립). 표 C: $(1, 4)$ 에서 $6 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 4 = 26$ (성립); $(2, 1)$ 에서 $6 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 1 = 17$ (성립); $(3, 2)$ 에서 $6 \\cdot 3 + 5 \\cdot 2 = 28$ (불성립). 표 D: $(1, 4)$ 에서 $6 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 4 = 26$ (성립); $(2, 1)$ 에서 $6 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 1 = 17$ (성립); $(3, 0)$ 에서 $6 \\cdot 3 + 5 \\cdot 0 = 18$ (성립). 세 행이 모두 성립하는 표는 D 하나뿐이므로 정답은 D이다.","explanation_en":"A table is correct only if every one of its rows satisfies $6c + 5d < 27$, that is, the left-hand quantity must be less than 27. Checking each row: Table A: $(1, 12)$ gives $6 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 12 = 66$ (fails); $(2, 7)$ gives $6 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 7 = 47$ (fails); $(3, 8)$ gives $6 \\cdot 3 + 5 \\cdot 8 = 58$ (fails). Table B: $(1, 4)$ gives $6 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 4 = 26$ (works); $(2, 3)$ gives $6 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 3 = 27$ (fails); $(3, 0)$ gives $6 \\cdot 3 + 5 \\cdot 0 = 18$ (works). Table C: $(1, 4)$ gives $6 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 4 = 26$ (works); $(2, 1)$ gives $6 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 1 = 17$ (works); $(3, 2)$ gives $6 \\cdot 3 + 5 \\cdot 2 = 28$ (fails). Table D: $(1, 4)$ gives $6 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 4 = 26$ (works); $(2, 1)$ gives $6 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 1 = 17$ (works); $(3, 0)$ gives $6 \\cdot 3 + 5 \\cdot 0 = 18$ (works). Only table D has all three rows satisfying the inequality, so the answer is D."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.258Z","issues":[],"alt":"그래프/도형 선택지 4개(A, B, C, D) — 같은 축·크기로 나란히."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-T1","fromVersionId":"0d91f902-bd4f-43e4-91fd-5c8f27e6fdbf","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-T2","problem_id":"37bdcded-f3e7-4a02-8f8c-077a6823aef5","from_version_id":"a9aea07c-a176-4c20-b6fa-5811598ee3c2","expect":{"explanation":"(1) 각 행의 x 로 2x + 6 를 계산한다. (2) y 가 그 값이상이다고 할 수 있는지 확인한다. (3) 정답 표의 각 행 (-2, 21), (-1, 10), (5, 16) 은 모두 조건을 만족한다. (4) 다른 표는 부등호 방향·경계·한 행·x 와 y 의 순서 중 하나가 어긋난다. 따라서 정답은 D이다.","explanation_en":"(1) Evaluate the right side. (2) Compare with y. (3) Every row of the correct table satisfies the condition. (4) Each other table fails in exactly one way. So the answer is D."},"set":{"explanation":"표가 정답이려면 모든 행이 부등식 $w \\ge 2r + 6$ 를 만족해야 한다. 즉 좌변의 값이 식의 값이상이어야 한다. 각 행을 대입하면 다음과 같다. 표 A: $(21, -2)$ 에서 $w = -2, 2 \\cdot 21 + 6 = 48$ (불성립); $(10, -1)$ 에서 $w = -1, 2 \\cdot 10 + 6 = 26$ (불성립); $(16, 5)$ 에서 $w = 5, 2 \\cdot 16 + 6 = 38$ (불성립). 표 B: $(-2, -10)$ 에서 $w = -10, 2 \\cdot (-2) + 6 = 2$ (불성립); $(-1, -12)$ 에서 $w = -12, 2 \\cdot (-1) + 6 = 4$ (불성립); $(5, 15)$ 에서 $w = 15, 2 \\cdot 5 + 6 = 16$ (불성립). 표 C: $(-2, 21)$ 에서 $w = 21, 2 \\cdot (-2) + 6 = 2$ (성립); $(-1, 10)$ 에서 $w = 10, 2 \\cdot (-1) + 6 = 4$ (성립); $(5, 11)$ 에서 $w = 11, 2 \\cdot 5 + 6 = 16$ (불성립). 표 D: $(-2, 21)$ 에서 $w = 21, 2 \\cdot (-2) + 6 = 2$ (성립); $(-1, 10)$ 에서 $w = 10, 2 \\cdot (-1) + 6 = 4$ (성립); $(5, 16)$ 에서 $w = 16, 2 \\cdot 5 + 6 = 16$ (성립). 세 행이 모두 성립하는 표는 D 하나뿐이므로 정답은 D이다.","explanation_en":"A table is correct only if every one of its rows satisfies $w \\ge 2r + 6$, that is, the left-hand quantity must be at least the expression. Checking each row: Table A: $(21, -2)$ gives $w = -2, 2 \\cdot 21 + 6 = 48$ (fails); $(10, -1)$ gives $w = -1, 2 \\cdot 10 + 6 = 26$ (fails); $(16, 5)$ gives $w = 5, 2 \\cdot 16 + 6 = 38$ (fails). Table B: $(-2, -10)$ gives $w = -10, 2 \\cdot (-2) + 6 = 2$ (fails); $(-1, -12)$ gives $w = -12, 2 \\cdot (-1) + 6 = 4$ (fails); $(5, 15)$ gives $w = 15, 2 \\cdot 5 + 6 = 16$ (fails). Table C: $(-2, 21)$ gives $w = 21, 2 \\cdot (-2) + 6 = 2$ (works); $(-1, 10)$ gives $w = 10, 2 \\cdot (-1) + 6 = 4$ (works); $(5, 11)$ gives $w = 11, 2 \\cdot 5 + 6 = 16$ (fails). Table D: $(-2, 21)$ gives $w = 21, 2 \\cdot (-2) + 6 = 2$ (works); $(-1, 10)$ gives $w = 10, 2 \\cdot (-1) + 6 = 4$ (works); $(5, 16)$ gives $w = 16, 2 \\cdot 5 + 6 = 16$ (works). Only table D has all three rows satisfying the inequality, so the answer is D."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.259Z","issues":[],"alt":"그래프/도형 선택지 4개(A, B, C, D) — 같은 축·크기로 나란히."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-T2","fromVersionId":"a9aea07c-a176-4c20-b6fa-5811598ee3c2","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-T3","problem_id":"4f9cd2de-22a0-4ec3-a23c-edb2bb73efd4","from_version_id":"92801793-7e0f-43f9-b46c-633ebfa500dd","expect":{"explanation":"(1) 각 행에서 3x + 5y 를 계산한다. (2) 그 값이 37 이상이다고 할 수 있는지 확인한다. (3) 경계(같은 경우)의 포함 여부도 확인한다. (4) 정답 표의 각 행 (0, 12), (1, 14), (7, 4) 은 모두 조건을 만족한다. (5) 다른 표는 부등호 방향·경계·한 행·x 와 y 의 순서 중 하나가 어긋난다. 따라서 정답은 C이다.","explanation_en":"(1) Compute the left side. (2) Compare with the constant. (3) Check the boundary rule. (4) Every row of the correct table satisfies the condition. (5) Each other table fails in exactly one way. So the answer is C."},"set":{"explanation":"표가 정답이려면 모든 행이 부등식 $3c + 5d \\ge 37$ 를 만족해야 한다. 즉 좌변의 값이 37이상이어야 한다. 각 행을 대입하면 다음과 같다. 표 A: $(0, 6)$ 에서 $3 \\cdot 0 + 5 \\cdot 6 = 30$ (불성립); $(1, 3)$ 에서 $3 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 3 = 18$ (불성립); $(7, 3)$ 에서 $3 \\cdot 7 + 5 \\cdot 3 = 36$ (불성립). 표 B: $(12, 0)$ 에서 $3 \\cdot 12 + 5 \\cdot 0 = 36$ (불성립); $(14, 1)$ 에서 $3 \\cdot 14 + 5 \\cdot 1 = 47$ (성립); $(4, 7)$ 에서 $3 \\cdot 4 + 5 \\cdot 7 = 47$ (성립). 표 C: $(0, 12)$ 에서 $3 \\cdot 0 + 5 \\cdot 12 = 60$ (성립); $(1, 14)$ 에서 $3 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 14 = 73$ (성립); $(7, 4)$ 에서 $3 \\cdot 7 + 5 \\cdot 4 = 41$ (성립). 표 D: $(0, 12)$ 에서 $3 \\cdot 0 + 5 \\cdot 12 = 60$ (성립); $(1, 14)$ 에서 $3 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 14 = 73$ (성립); $(7, 2)$ 에서 $3 \\cdot 7 + 5 \\cdot 2 = 31$ (불성립). 세 행이 모두 성립하는 표는 C 하나뿐이므로 정답은 C이다.","explanation_en":"A table is correct only if every one of its rows satisfies $3c + 5d \\ge 37$, that is, the left-hand quantity must be at least 37. Checking each row: Table A: $(0, 6)$ gives $3 \\cdot 0 + 5 \\cdot 6 = 30$ (fails); $(1, 3)$ gives $3 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 3 = 18$ (fails); $(7, 3)$ gives $3 \\cdot 7 + 5 \\cdot 3 = 36$ (fails). Table B: $(12, 0)$ gives $3 \\cdot 12 + 5 \\cdot 0 = 36$ (fails); $(14, 1)$ gives $3 \\cdot 14 + 5 \\cdot 1 = 47$ (works); $(4, 7)$ gives $3 \\cdot 4 + 5 \\cdot 7 = 47$ (works). Table C: $(0, 12)$ gives $3 \\cdot 0 + 5 \\cdot 12 = 60$ (works); $(1, 14)$ gives $3 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 14 = 73$ (works); $(7, 4)$ gives $3 \\cdot 7 + 5 \\cdot 4 = 41$ (works). Table D: $(0, 12)$ gives $3 \\cdot 0 + 5 \\cdot 12 = 60$ (works); $(1, 14)$ gives $3 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\cdot 14 = 73$ (works); $(7, 2)$ gives $3 \\cdot 7 + 5 \\cdot 2 = 31$ (fails). Only table C has all three rows satisfying the inequality, so the answer is C."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.261Z","issues":[],"alt":"그래프/도형 선택지 4개(A, B, C, D) — 같은 축·크기로 나란히."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-T3","fromVersionId":"92801793-7e0f-43f9-b46c-633ebfa500dd","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-T4","problem_id":"82013842-74a9-459a-aa34-8e17b558b025","from_version_id":"81316787-81f3-4fa5-a294-9409605248ec","expect":{"explanation":"(1) 총비용은 12x + 5y 이다. (2) 'at most' 이므로 12x + 5y <= 96 이다. (3) 각 표의 행마다 12x + 5y 를 계산해 96 와 비교한다. (4) 정답 표의 각 행 (2, 14), (5, 0), (6, 0) 은 모두 조건을 만족한다. (5) 다른 표는 부등호 방향·경계·한 행·x 와 y 의 순서 중 하나가 어긋난다. 따라서 정답은 D이다.","explanation_en":"(1) Write the total cost. (2) Translate the comparison word into an inequality sign. (3) Substitute each row. (4) Every row of the correct table satisfies the condition. (5) Each other table fails in exactly one way. So the answer is D."},"set":{"explanation":"표가 정답이려면 모든 행이 부등식 $12u + 5v \\le 96$ 를 만족해야 한다. 즉 좌변의 값이 96이하여야 한다. 각 행을 대입하면 다음과 같다. 표 A: $(14, 2)$ 에서 $12 \\cdot 14 + 5 \\cdot 2 = 178$ (불성립); $(0, 5)$ 에서 $12 \\cdot 0 + 5 \\cdot 5 = 25$ (성립); $(0, 6)$ 에서 $12 \\cdot 0 + 5 \\cdot 6 = 30$ (성립). 표 B: $(2, 21)$ 에서 $12 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 21 = 129$ (불성립); $(5, 10)$ 에서 $12 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot 10 = 110$ (불성립); $(6, 22)$ 에서 $12 \\cdot 6 + 5 \\cdot 22 = 182$ (불성립). 표 C: $(2, 14)$ 에서 $12 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 14 = 94$ (성립); $(5, 0)$ 에서 $12 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot 0 = 60$ (성립); $(6, 7)$ 에서 $12 \\cdot 6 + 5 \\cdot 7 = 107$ (불성립). 표 D: $(2, 14)$ 에서 $12 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 14 = 94$ (성립); $(5, 0)$ 에서 $12 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot 0 = 60$ (성립); $(6, 0)$ 에서 $12 \\cdot 6 + 5 \\cdot 0 = 72$ (성립). 세 행이 모두 성립하는 표는 D 하나뿐이므로 정답은 D이다.","explanation_en":"A table is correct only if every one of its rows satisfies $12u + 5v \\le 96$, that is, the left-hand quantity must be at most 96. Checking each row: Table A: $(14, 2)$ gives $12 \\cdot 14 + 5 \\cdot 2 = 178$ (fails); $(0, 5)$ gives $12 \\cdot 0 + 5 \\cdot 5 = 25$ (works); $(0, 6)$ gives $12 \\cdot 0 + 5 \\cdot 6 = 30$ (works). Table B: $(2, 21)$ gives $12 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 21 = 129$ (fails); $(5, 10)$ gives $12 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot 10 = 110$ (fails); $(6, 22)$ gives $12 \\cdot 6 + 5 \\cdot 22 = 182$ (fails). Table C: $(2, 14)$ gives $12 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 14 = 94$ (works); $(5, 0)$ gives $12 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot 0 = 60$ (works); $(6, 7)$ gives $12 \\cdot 6 + 5 \\cdot 7 = 107$ (fails). Table D: $(2, 14)$ gives $12 \\cdot 2 + 5 \\cdot 14 = 94$ (works); $(5, 0)$ gives $12 \\cdot 5 + 5 \\cdot 0 = 60$ (works); $(6, 0)$ gives $12 \\cdot 6 + 5 \\cdot 0 = 72$ (works). Only table D has all three rows satisfying the inequality, so the answer is D."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.261Z","issues":[],"alt":"그래프/도형 선택지 4개(A, B, C, D) — 같은 축·크기로 나란히."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-T4","fromVersionId":"81316787-81f3-4fa5-a294-9409605248ec","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-T5","problem_id":"9279fb81-2f88-43e5-9b68-8f4933a3b0ea","from_version_id":"fc38fb13-5025-41ea-b47e-85d9a953e65e","expect":{"explanation":"(1) 각 행에서 4x + 4y 를 계산한다. (2) 그 값이 36 보다 작다고 할 수 있는지 확인한다. (3) 경계(같은 경우)의 포함 여부도 확인한다. (4) 정답 표의 각 행 (3, 5), (4, 3), (5, 0) 은 모두 조건을 만족한다. (5) 다른 표는 부등호 방향·경계·한 행·x 와 y 의 순서 중 하나가 어긋난다. 따라서 정답은 C이다.","explanation_en":"(1) Compute the left side. (2) Compare with the constant. (3) Check the boundary rule. (4) Every row of the correct table satisfies the condition. (5) Each other table fails in exactly one way. So the answer is C."},"set":{"explanation":"표가 정답이려면 모든 행이 부등식 $4r + 4w < 36$ 를 만족해야 한다. 즉 좌변의 값이 36보다 작아야 한다. 각 행을 대입하면 다음과 같다. 표 A: $(3, 5)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 3 + 4 \\cdot 5 = 32$ (성립); $(4, 5)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 4 + 4 \\cdot 5 = 36$ (불성립); $(5, 0)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 5 + 4 \\cdot 0 = 20$ (성립). 표 B: $(3, 11)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 3 + 4 \\cdot 11 = 56$ (불성립); $(4, 10)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 4 + 4 \\cdot 10 = 56$ (불성립); $(5, 12)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 5 + 4 \\cdot 12 = 68$ (불성립). 표 C: $(3, 5)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 3 + 4 \\cdot 5 = 32$ (성립); $(4, 3)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 4 + 4 \\cdot 3 = 28$ (성립); $(5, 0)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 5 + 4 \\cdot 0 = 20$ (성립). 표 D: $(3, 5)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 3 + 4 \\cdot 5 = 32$ (성립); $(4, 3)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 4 + 4 \\cdot 3 = 28$ (성립); $(5, 5)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 5 + 4 \\cdot 5 = 40$ (불성립). 세 행이 모두 성립하는 표는 C 하나뿐이므로 정답은 C이다.","explanation_en":"A table is correct only if every one of its rows satisfies $4r + 4w < 36$, that is, the left-hand quantity must be less than 36. Checking each row: Table A: $(3, 5)$ gives $4 \\cdot 3 + 4 \\cdot 5 = 32$ (works); $(4, 5)$ gives $4 \\cdot 4 + 4 \\cdot 5 = 36$ (fails); $(5, 0)$ gives $4 \\cdot 5 + 4 \\cdot 0 = 20$ (works). Table B: $(3, 11)$ gives $4 \\cdot 3 + 4 \\cdot 11 = 56$ (fails); $(4, 10)$ gives $4 \\cdot 4 + 4 \\cdot 10 = 56$ (fails); $(5, 12)$ gives $4 \\cdot 5 + 4 \\cdot 12 = 68$ (fails). Table C: $(3, 5)$ gives $4 \\cdot 3 + 4 \\cdot 5 = 32$ (works); $(4, 3)$ gives $4 \\cdot 4 + 4 \\cdot 3 = 28$ (works); $(5, 0)$ gives $4 \\cdot 5 + 4 \\cdot 0 = 20$ (works). Table D: $(3, 5)$ gives $4 \\cdot 3 + 4 \\cdot 5 = 32$ (works); $(4, 3)$ gives $4 \\cdot 4 + 4 \\cdot 3 = 28$ (works); $(5, 5)$ gives $4 \\cdot 5 + 4 \\cdot 5 = 40$ (fails). Only table C has all three rows satisfying the inequality, so the answer is C."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.262Z","issues":[],"alt":"그래프/도형 선택지 4개(A, B, C, D) — 같은 축·크기로 나란히."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-T5","fromVersionId":"fc38fb13-5025-41ea-b47e-85d9a953e65e","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-T6","problem_id":"9371a31e-239c-44bf-9831-8b184767c145","from_version_id":"e5a54e8d-5922-45c5-ba6d-5c163ccfb847","expect":{"explanation":"(1) 각 행에서 4x + 2y 를 계산한다. (2) 그 값이 25 이하이다고 할 수 있는지 확인한다. (3) 경계(같은 경우)의 포함 여부도 확인한다. (4) 정답 표의 각 행 (0, 7), (4, 4), (5, 2) 은 모두 조건을 만족한다. (5) 다른 표는 부등호 방향·경계·한 행·x 와 y 의 순서 중 하나가 어긋난다. 따라서 정답은 A이다.","explanation_en":"(1) Compute the left side. (2) Compare with the constant. (3) Check the boundary rule. (4) Every row of the correct table satisfies the condition. (5) Each other table fails in exactly one way. So the answer is A."},"set":{"explanation":"표가 정답이려면 모든 행이 부등식 $4a + 2b \\le 25$ 를 만족해야 한다. 즉 좌변의 값이 25이하여야 한다. 각 행을 대입하면 다음과 같다. 표 A: $(0, 7)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 0 + 2 \\cdot 7 = 14$ (성립); $(4, 4)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 4 = 24$ (성립); $(5, 2)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\cdot 2 = 24$ (성립). 표 B: $(0, 7)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 0 + 2 \\cdot 7 = 14$ (성립); $(4, 4)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 4 = 24$ (성립); $(5, 5)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\cdot 5 = 30$ (불성립). 표 C: $(0, 16)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 0 + 2 \\cdot 16 = 32$ (불성립); $(4, 7)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 7 = 30$ (불성립); $(5, 19)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\cdot 19 = 58$ (불성립). 표 D: $(7, 0)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 7 + 2 \\cdot 0 = 28$ (불성립); $(4, 4)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 4 = 24$ (성립); $(2, 5)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 5 = 18$ (성립). 세 행이 모두 성립하는 표는 A 하나뿐이므로 정답은 A이다.","explanation_en":"A table is correct only if every one of its rows satisfies $4a + 2b \\le 25$, that is, the left-hand quantity must be at most 25. Checking each row: Table A: $(0, 7)$ gives $4 \\cdot 0 + 2 \\cdot 7 = 14$ (works); $(4, 4)$ gives $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 4 = 24$ (works); $(5, 2)$ gives $4 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\cdot 2 = 24$ (works). Table B: $(0, 7)$ gives $4 \\cdot 0 + 2 \\cdot 7 = 14$ (works); $(4, 4)$ gives $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 4 = 24$ (works); $(5, 5)$ gives $4 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\cdot 5 = 30$ (fails). Table C: $(0, 16)$ gives $4 \\cdot 0 + 2 \\cdot 16 = 32$ (fails); $(4, 7)$ gives $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 7 = 30$ (fails); $(5, 19)$ gives $4 \\cdot 5 + 2 \\cdot 19 = 58$ (fails). Table D: $(7, 0)$ gives $4 \\cdot 7 + 2 \\cdot 0 = 28$ (fails); $(4, 4)$ gives $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 4 = 24$ (works); $(2, 5)$ gives $4 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 5 = 18$ (works). Only table A has all three rows satisfying the inequality, so the answer is A."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.263Z","issues":[],"alt":"그래프/도형 선택지 4개(A, B, C, D) — 같은 축·크기로 나란히."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-T6","fromVersionId":"e5a54e8d-5922-45c5-ba6d-5c163ccfb847","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-T7","problem_id":"c8ad79f8-a367-4848-b48c-daa56bada132","from_version_id":"6f48ec73-18ad-4fbc-96f5-042b775c14b6","expect":{"explanation":"(1) 각 행에서 4x + 2y 를 계산한다. (2) 그 값이 24 보다 크다고 할 수 있는지 확인한다. (3) 경계(같은 경우)의 포함 여부도 확인한다. (4) 정답 표의 각 행 (2, 12), (3, 12), (4, 5) 은 모두 조건을 만족한다. (5) 다른 표는 부등호 방향·경계·한 행·x 와 y 의 순서 중 하나가 어긋난다. 따라서 정답은 C이다.","explanation_en":"(1) Compute the left side. (2) Compare with the constant. (3) Check the boundary rule. (4) Every row of the correct table satisfies the condition. (5) Each other table fails in exactly one way. So the answer is C."},"set":{"explanation":"표가 정답이려면 모든 행이 부등식 $4c + 2d > 24$ 를 만족해야 한다. 즉 좌변의 값이 24보다 커야 한다. 각 행을 대입하면 다음과 같다. 표 A: $(2, 0)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 0 = 8$ (불성립); $(3, 4)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 3 + 2 \\cdot 4 = 20$ (불성립); $(4, 0)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 0 = 16$ (불성립). 표 B: $(2, 12)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 12 = 32$ (성립); $(3, 12)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 3 + 2 \\cdot 12 = 36$ (성립); $(4, 4)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 4 = 24$ (불성립). 표 C: $(2, 12)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 12 = 32$ (성립); $(3, 12)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 3 + 2 \\cdot 12 = 36$ (성립); $(4, 5)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 5 = 26$ (성립). 표 D: $(2, 12)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 12 = 32$ (성립); $(3, 4)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 3 + 2 \\cdot 4 = 20$ (불성립); $(4, 5)$ 에서 $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 5 = 26$ (성립). 세 행이 모두 성립하는 표는 C 하나뿐이므로 정답은 C이다.","explanation_en":"A table is correct only if every one of its rows satisfies $4c + 2d > 24$, that is, the left-hand quantity must be greater than 24. Checking each row: Table A: $(2, 0)$ gives $4 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 0 = 8$ (fails); $(3, 4)$ gives $4 \\cdot 3 + 2 \\cdot 4 = 20$ (fails); $(4, 0)$ gives $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 0 = 16$ (fails). Table B: $(2, 12)$ gives $4 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 12 = 32$ (works); $(3, 12)$ gives $4 \\cdot 3 + 2 \\cdot 12 = 36$ (works); $(4, 4)$ gives $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 4 = 24$ (fails). Table C: $(2, 12)$ gives $4 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 12 = 32$ (works); $(3, 12)$ gives $4 \\cdot 3 + 2 \\cdot 12 = 36$ (works); $(4, 5)$ gives $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 5 = 26$ (works). Table D: $(2, 12)$ gives $4 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 12 = 32$ (works); $(3, 4)$ gives $4 \\cdot 3 + 2 \\cdot 4 = 20$ (fails); $(4, 5)$ gives $4 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 5 = 26$ (works). Only table C has all three rows satisfying the inequality, so the answer is C."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.264Z","issues":[],"alt":"그래프/도형 선택지 4개(A, B, C, D) — 같은 축·크기로 나란히."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-T7","fromVersionId":"6f48ec73-18ad-4fbc-96f5-042b775c14b6","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-T8","problem_id":"d61e5b77-10c1-481d-b1be-34d5f3395a46","from_version_id":"db18791e-c778-40b1-9490-0851be0b6dfb","expect":{"explanation":"(1) 각 행의 x 로 4x - 4 를 계산한다. (2) y 가 그 값보다 작다고 할 수 있는지 확인한다. (3) 정답 표의 각 행 (-2, -18), (1, -8), (5, 15) 은 모두 조건을 만족한다. (4) 다른 표는 부등호 방향·경계·한 행·x 와 y 의 순서 중 하나가 어긋난다. 따라서 정답은 A이다.","explanation_en":"(1) Evaluate the right side. (2) Compare with y. (3) Every row of the correct table satisfies the condition. (4) Each other table fails in exactly one way. So the answer is A."},"set":{"explanation":"표가 정답이려면 모든 행이 부등식 $v < 4u - 4$ 를 만족해야 한다. 즉 좌변의 값이 식의 값보다 작아야 한다. 각 행을 대입하면 다음과 같다. 표 A: $(-2, -18)$ 에서 $v = -18, 4 \\cdot (-2) - 4 = -12$ (성립); $(1, -8)$ 에서 $v = -8, 4 \\cdot 1 - 4 = 0$ (성립); $(5, 15)$ 에서 $v = 15, 4 \\cdot 5 - 4 = 16$ (성립). 표 B: $(-18, -2)$ 에서 $v = -2, 4 \\cdot (-18) - 4 = -76$ (불성립); $(-8, 1)$ 에서 $v = 1, 4 \\cdot (-8) - 4 = -36$ (불성립); $(15, 5)$ 에서 $v = 5, 4 \\cdot 15 - 4 = 56$ (성립). 표 C: $(-2, -12)$ 에서 $v = -12, 4 \\cdot (-2) - 4 = -12$ (불성립); $(1, -8)$ 에서 $v = -8, 4 \\cdot 1 - 4 = 0$ (성립); $(5, 15)$ 에서 $v = 15, 4 \\cdot 5 - 4 = 16$ (성립). 표 D: $(-2, -18)$ 에서 $v = -18, 4 \\cdot (-2) - 4 = -12$ (성립); $(1, 3)$ 에서 $v = 3, 4 \\cdot 1 - 4 = 0$ (불성립); $(5, 15)$ 에서 $v = 15, 4 \\cdot 5 - 4 = 16$ (성립). 세 행이 모두 성립하는 표는 A 하나뿐이므로 정답은 A이다.","explanation_en":"A table is correct only if every one of its rows satisfies $v < 4u - 4$, that is, the left-hand quantity must be less than the expression. Checking each row: Table A: $(-2, -18)$ gives $v = -18, 4 \\cdot (-2) - 4 = -12$ (works); $(1, -8)$ gives $v = -8, 4 \\cdot 1 - 4 = 0$ (works); $(5, 15)$ gives $v = 15, 4 \\cdot 5 - 4 = 16$ (works). Table B: $(-18, -2)$ gives $v = -2, 4 \\cdot (-18) - 4 = -76$ (fails); $(-8, 1)$ gives $v = 1, 4 \\cdot (-8) - 4 = -36$ (fails); $(15, 5)$ gives $v = 5, 4 \\cdot 15 - 4 = 56$ (works). Table C: $(-2, -12)$ gives $v = -12, 4 \\cdot (-2) - 4 = -12$ (fails); $(1, -8)$ gives $v = -8, 4 \\cdot 1 - 4 = 0$ (works); $(5, 15)$ gives $v = 15, 4 \\cdot 5 - 4 = 16$ (works). Table D: $(-2, -18)$ gives $v = -18, 4 \\cdot (-2) - 4 = -12$ (works); $(1, 3)$ gives $v = 3, 4 \\cdot 1 - 4 = 0$ (fails); $(5, 15)$ gives $v = 15, 4 \\cdot 5 - 4 = 16$ (works). Only table A has all three rows satisfying the inequality, so the answer is A."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.264Z","issues":[],"alt":"그래프/도형 선택지 4개(A, B, C, D) — 같은 축·크기로 나란히."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-T8","fromVersionId":"db18791e-c778-40b1-9490-0851be0b6dfb","fromVersionNo":1}},{"patch_id":"R2-T9","problem_id":"e05784b4-479a-4fe1-b9ab-68cb41be5af6","from_version_id":"4b9f25fd-7208-440b-b275-fa5ef79cf651","expect":{"explanation":"(1) 각 행에서 2x + 4y 를 계산한다. (2) 그 값이 18 이상이다고 할 수 있는지 확인한다. (3) 경계(같은 경우)의 포함 여부도 확인한다. (4) 정답 표의 각 행 (2, 4), (7, 1), (8, 9) 은 모두 조건을 만족한다. (5) 다른 표는 부등호 방향·경계·한 행·x 와 y 의 순서 중 하나가 어긋난다. 따라서 정답은 C이다.","explanation_en":"(1) Compute the left side. (2) Compare with the constant. (3) Check the boundary rule. (4) Every row of the correct table satisfies the condition. (5) Each other table fails in exactly one way. So the answer is C."},"set":{"explanation":"표가 정답이려면 모든 행이 부등식 $2r + 4w \\ge 18$ 를 만족해야 한다. 즉 좌변의 값이 18이상이어야 한다. 각 행을 대입하면 다음과 같다. 표 A: $(2, 2)$ 에서 $2 \\cdot 2 + 4 \\cdot 2 = 12$ (불성립); $(7, 0)$ 에서 $2 \\cdot 7 + 4 \\cdot 0 = 14$ (불성립); $(8, 0)$ 에서 $2 \\cdot 8 + 4 \\cdot 0 = 16$ (불성립). 표 B: $(2, 4)$ 에서 $2 \\cdot 2 + 4 \\cdot 4 = 20$ (성립); $(7, 0)$ 에서 $2 \\cdot 7 + 4 \\cdot 0 = 14$ (불성립); $(8, 9)$ 에서 $2 \\cdot 8 + 4 \\cdot 9 = 52$ (성립). 표 C: $(2, 4)$ 에서 $2 \\cdot 2 + 4 \\cdot 4 = 20$ (성립); $(7, 1)$ 에서 $2 \\cdot 7 + 4 \\cdot 1 = 18$ (성립); $(8, 9)$ 에서 $2 \\cdot 8 + 4 \\cdot 9 = 52$ (성립). 표 D: $(4, 2)$ 에서 $2 \\cdot 4 + 4 \\cdot 2 = 16$ (불성립); $(1, 7)$ 에서 $2 \\cdot 1 + 4 \\cdot 7 = 30$ (성립); $(9, 8)$ 에서 $2 \\cdot 9 + 4 \\cdot 8 = 50$ (성립). 세 행이 모두 성립하는 표는 C 하나뿐이므로 정답은 C이다.","explanation_en":"A table is correct only if every one of its rows satisfies $2r + 4w \\ge 18$, that is, the left-hand quantity must be at least 18. Checking each row: Table A: $(2, 2)$ gives $2 \\cdot 2 + 4 \\cdot 2 = 12$ (fails); $(7, 0)$ gives $2 \\cdot 7 + 4 \\cdot 0 = 14$ (fails); $(8, 0)$ gives $2 \\cdot 8 + 4 \\cdot 0 = 16$ (fails). Table B: $(2, 4)$ gives $2 \\cdot 2 + 4 \\cdot 4 = 20$ (works); $(7, 0)$ gives $2 \\cdot 7 + 4 \\cdot 0 = 14$ (fails); $(8, 9)$ gives $2 \\cdot 8 + 4 \\cdot 9 = 52$ (works). Table C: $(2, 4)$ gives $2 \\cdot 2 + 4 \\cdot 4 = 20$ (works); $(7, 1)$ gives $2 \\cdot 7 + 4 \\cdot 1 = 18$ (works); $(8, 9)$ gives $2 \\cdot 8 + 4 \\cdot 9 = 52$ (works). Table D: $(4, 2)$ gives $2 \\cdot 4 + 4 \\cdot 2 = 16$ (fails); $(1, 7)$ gives $2 \\cdot 1 + 4 \\cdot 7 = 30$ (works); $(9, 8)$ gives $2 \\cdot 9 + 4 \\cdot 8 = 50$ (works). Only table C has all three rows satisfying the inequality, so the answer is C."},"render_check":{"ok":true,"renderer":"std-1","checkedAt":"2026-10-14T00:38:57.265Z","issues":[],"alt":"그래프/도형 선택지 4개(A, B, C, D) — 같은 축·크기로 나란히."},"audit_fix":{"source":"external_audit_2026-10-13","patch":"R2-T9","fromVersionId":"4b9f25fd-7208-440b-b275-fa5ef79cf651","fromVersionNo":1}}],"sets":[{"set_group_id":"693168bd-3fb0-4cdf-8dc4-21186e80cf39","set_id":"14fbe2e4-662a-4203-8f4a-a3a9822733c2","name":"SAT Practice Test 1","version_no":3,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["8f837ceb-4c51-4a7c-b3ed-2955cdc1f652"],"patch_ids":["R2-M24"]},{"set_group_id":"4ed98082-6042-41bb-9c3c-1fc645f5d463","set_id":"8995fe40-bb9c-4eb2-9659-1924f1e51642","name":"SAT Practice Test 10","version_no":2,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["37bdcded-f3e7-4a02-8f8c-077a6823aef5"],"patch_ids":["R2-T2"]},{"set_group_id":"8deac77d-54b2-4071-8139-613c84c69c6c","set_id":"e16b02e7-5ca3-43ba-a0ae-013e906ec923","name":"SAT Practice Test 11","version_no":2,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["40c78fe3-ff66-4f3e-b4eb-12837f9ef8e2","9543e92d-f90c-4314-bfa1-50d02d356866","4f9cd2de-22a0-4ec3-a23c-edb2bb73efd4"],"patch_ids":["R2-P05-fern","R2-P05-ngc","R2-T3"]},{"set_group_id":"11ca1e4b-a6e6-446a-821e-1a2ad2bd1a78","set_id":"aef0dde9-ce35-4265-9ba8-fa1d142bc9ad","name":"SAT Practice Test 12","version_no":2,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["9371a31e-239c-44bf-9831-8b184767c145"],"patch_ids":["R2-T6"]},{"set_group_id":"81373039-c769-4d74-93f0-8bb1da0e26ee","set_id":"f4bb2202-3861-4f0e-baba-d14ae5eede0e","name":"SAT Practice Test 13","version_no":2,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["359548c6-2d67-4934-92cf-58d5e429bff2","212ae011-ef9e-4cdc-9cf6-361cf9c8006e","c8ad79f8-a367-4848-b48c-daa56bada132"],"patch_ids":["R2-M19","R2-M20","R2-T7"]},{"set_group_id":"f4f02828-f657-467c-91a2-169955e44869","set_id":"dd7943f7-dd48-45dd-8479-805c76cd28ed","name":"SAT Practice Test 2","version_no":3,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["197fb7f1-4641-4965-ac6f-03a91db8088b","8b49717e-5b2f-45ea-8a6f-e3478b914095","318348e4-6f29-4a78-92ec-2e2d427f5c54"],"patch_ids":["R2-G10a","R2-G10b","R2-SCAN-318348e4"]},{"set_group_id":"a9e69f00-5935-412d-8b42-07cd8438b040","set_id":"c71e9b93-7668-4f02-8b28-5934db648e97","name":"SAT Practice Test 3","version_no":3,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["3c327f82-85da-4467-9209-bc8dfc5256ff","ef903982-122d-41bf-a83c-9de904e8c159","eadbd9ab-c1ce-4f9f-92fd-5d3de35cc2b3","0cdf0650-bc5c-4dc5-ab5a-2efa21e968a2"],"patch_ids":["R2-G07","R2-G08","R2-M23","R2-P04"]},{"set_group_id":"976dfc8b-c610-41e8-ac72-d0715fd697cd","set_id":"e88d2950-123f-4c71-adb2-e03f12fc9d43","name":"SAT Practice Test 4","version_no":3,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["0049d229-8bdc-4a0d-a585-c43cbe9e96dd","b4df4856-bad0-42c1-9d7d-e02f0df9274c","4c9ee2b7-687b-43c6-a04d-52448f943950","59115b48-1695-4f87-a971-4b741373d0ec","00b041df-ea9a-4a4b-a1a3-8fccd7fbc39f"],"patch_ids":["R2-M17","R2-P02","R2-P03","R2-P05-sponge","R2-T1"]},{"set_group_id":"e1d076eb-e2c5-4a80-b91a-875dc24988f3","set_id":"400024a3-c0b8-4e18-a371-ffdbccca6a50","name":"SAT Practice Test 5","version_no":3,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["b7c3a411-636e-4408-82ca-2c39be73e605","8d4393d8-8f93-40d6-9122-0f1b16ac7f4e"],"patch_ids":["R2-G11a","R2-G11b"]},{"set_group_id":"dbe9ac87-7fb4-4413-bc68-2ba959b405ea","set_id":"580d0846-29dd-445e-934d-d66043fc815e","name":"SAT Practice Test 6","version_no":3,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["65748760-4afd-4506-9289-a5e1f9af6f9e","4bcb7479-58a7-4110-8d2d-e2b70e5a8928"],"patch_ids":["R2-G12","R2-M25"]},{"set_group_id":"b7c2f1c4-b6a7-432c-b56a-b7329ca9f851","set_id":"45d7ffdf-f74d-4a5d-a5d2-82a12b986695","name":"SAT Practice Test 7","version_no":3,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["fa015adb-185f-4b33-ae6a-5de398a1143e","a422e2b1-ade3-48b9-90d3-7013a98f6803","abc08190-cb4e-4189-a3e6-325fb165a973"],"patch_ids":["R2-P05-bridge","R2-N01","R2-N02"]},{"set_group_id":"d572c4b4-0391-47ec-88e7-ef6a345ffc20","set_id":"facbffc8-167b-4de6-b4c1-fc1c635803d9","name":"SAT Practice Test 8","version_no":3,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["831408a6-08f4-413f-8727-81c32568ae8a","8614efec-7183-4191-af98-39f618153e03"],"patch_ids":["R2-G13","R2-P05-fern2"]},{"set_group_id":"ed2c2f97-b810-4136-9b8e-4aa2113d7309","set_id":"2608e0ac-fdae-493c-a62d-edcb66d2d5c6","name":"SAT Practice Test 9","version_no":3,"format":"mst","problem_ids":["52cd5e73-5a6b-4779-ae38-d81ecc8df996"],"patch_ids":["R2-P05-frb"]}]}$audit_plan$::jsonb;
  v_actor uuid := (select id from profiles where role = 'admin' order by created_at limit 1);
  u jsonb; s jsonb; a jsonb;
  v_prob problems%rowtype; v_cur problem_versions%rowtype; v_have jsonb; v_want jsonb; v_new uuid;
  v_src mock_exam_sets%rowtype; v_new_set uuid; v_cols text; v_cols_i text; v_pids uuid[];
  v_n int; v_stale int; v_bad text; v_done_p int := 0; v_skip_p int := 0; v_done_s int := 0; v_skip_s int := 0;
begin
  if v_actor is null then raise exception 'No admin profile found.'; end if;

  -- Phase 1: problems -> new published problem version each (same flow as the admin problem editor)
  for u in select * from jsonb_array_elements(v_plan->'problems') loop
    select * into v_prob from problems where id = (u->>'problem_id')::uuid;
    if v_prob.id is null then raise exception '[%] problem % not found', u->>'patch_id', u->>'problem_id'; end if;
    select * into v_cur from problem_versions where id = v_prob.published_version_id;
    if v_cur.id is null then raise exception '[%] problem % has no published version', u->>'patch_id', v_prob.id; end if;
    -- final field values = current published version, overridden by the planned new values
    a := jsonb_build_object('passage', v_cur.passage, 'question', v_cur.question, 'options', v_cur.options, 'correct_index', v_cur.correct_index, 'answers', v_cur.answers,
                            'explanation', v_cur.explanation, 'explanation_en', v_cur.explanation_en, 'figure', v_cur.figure, 'statements', v_cur.statements, 'difficulty', v_cur.difficulty,
                            'figure_checked', coalesce(v_cur.figure_checked, false), 'evidence_target', v_cur.evidence_target, 'evidence_span', v_cur.evidence_span,
                            'answer_rationale', v_cur.answer_rationale, 'distractor_error_types', v_cur.distractor_error_types);
    v_have := (select coalesce(jsonb_object_agg(k, a->k), '{}'::jsonb) from jsonb_object_keys(u->'expect') k);
    v_want := (select coalesce(jsonb_object_agg(k, (a || (u->'set'))->k), '{}'::jsonb) from jsonb_object_keys(u->'expect') k);
    if v_have = v_want then v_skip_p := v_skip_p + 1; raise notice '[%] already applied, skipped', u->>'patch_id'; continue; end if;
    if v_have <> u->'expect' then raise exception '[%] DRIFT: the published version of problem % no longer matches the expected old content (re-run the dry-run)', u->>'patch_id', v_prob.id; end if;
    a := a || (u->'set');
    if exists (select 1 from problem_versions where problem_id = v_prob.id and status in ('draft', 'in_review')) then
      raise exception '[%] problem % has an open draft/in_review version', u->>'patch_id', v_prob.id;
    end if;
    v_new := public.save_problem_draft_version(
      p_problem_id => v_prob.id, p_passage => a->>'passage', p_options => nullif(a->'options', 'null'::jsonb), p_correct_index => (a->>'correct_index')::int,
      p_explanation => a->>'explanation', p_difficulty => a->>'difficulty', p_actor_id => v_actor, p_answers => nullif(a->'answers', 'null'::jsonb),
      p_figure => nullif(a->'figure', 'null'::jsonb), p_figure_checked => coalesce((a->>'figure_checked')::boolean, false), p_statements => nullif(a->'statements', 'null'::jsonb),
      p_question => a->>'question', p_repair_status => null, p_explanation_en => a->>'explanation_en', p_evidence_target => a->>'evidence_target',
      p_evidence_span => a->>'evidence_span', p_answer_rationale => a->>'answer_rationale', p_distractor_error_types => nullif(a->'distractor_error_types', 'null'::jsonb));
    perform public.set_problem_render_check(v_new, u->'render_check');
    perform public.set_problem_quality(v_new, coalesce(v_cur.quality, '{}'::jsonb) || jsonb_build_object('auditFix', u->'audit_fix'));
    perform public.confirm_and_publish_problem_version(v_new, v_actor);
    if u ? 'ap' then perform public.ap_attach_new_version(u->'ap'->>'candidate_key', (u->'ap'->>'item_index')::int, v_new, v_actor); end if;
    v_done_p := v_done_p + 1;
    raise notice '[%] problem % -> new version %', u->>'patch_id', v_prob.id, v_new;
  end loop;

  -- Phase 2: sets -> new set version per affected published set (items of the patched problems point to the new problem versions; snapshots of the rest are copied)
  for s in select * from jsonb_array_elements(v_plan->'sets') loop
    select array_agg(x::uuid) into v_pids from jsonb_array_elements_text(s->'problem_ids') x;
    select * into v_src from mock_exam_sets where set_group_id = (s->>'set_group_id')::uuid and status = 'published' and archived_at is null order by version_no desc limit 1 for update;
    if v_src.id is null then raise exception 'Set group % has no published version (%).', s->>'set_group_id', s->>'name'; end if;
    select count(*) into v_n from mock_exam_set_items i where i.exam_set_id = v_src.id and i.problem_id = any (v_pids);
    if v_n <> cardinality(v_pids) then raise exception 'Set % v% holds % of % target problems.', v_src.name, v_src.version_no, v_n, cardinality(v_pids); end if;
    select count(*) into v_stale from mock_exam_set_items i join problems p on p.id = i.problem_id
     where i.exam_set_id = v_src.id and i.problem_id = any (v_pids) and p.published_version_id is distinct from i.problem_version_id;
    if v_stale = 0 then v_skip_s := v_skip_s + 1; raise notice 'set % v% already up to date, skipped', v_src.name, v_src.version_no; continue; end if;
    perform pg_advisory_xact_lock(hashtextextended('mock_exam_new_version:' || v_src.set_group_id::text, 0));
    if exists (select 1 from mock_exam_sets where set_group_id = v_src.set_group_id and status = 'draft') then
      raise exception 'A draft version already exists for set % - publish or archive it first.', v_src.name;
    end if;
    v_new_set := gen_random_uuid();
    select string_agg(quote_ident(column_name), ', ' order by ordinal_position) into v_cols
      from information_schema.columns where table_schema = 'public' and table_name = 'mock_exam_sets' and is_generated = 'NEVER'
       and column_name not in ('id', 'version_no', 'status', 'created_by', 'created_at', 'published_at', 'published_by', 'archived_at', 'access_tier');
    execute format('insert into mock_exam_sets (id, version_no, status, created_by, access_tier, %s) select $1, $2, ''draft'', $3, ''tutoring'', %s from mock_exam_sets where id = $4', v_cols, v_cols)
      using v_new_set, (select max(version_no) + 1 from mock_exam_sets where set_group_id = v_src.set_group_id), v_actor, v_src.id;
    select string_agg(quote_ident(column_name), ', ' order by ordinal_position),
           string_agg(case column_name
                        when 'problem_version_id' then 'case when i.problem_id = any ($3) then coalesce(p.published_version_id, i.problem_version_id) else i.problem_version_id end'
                        when 'content_snapshot' then 'case when i.problem_id = any ($3) then null else i.content_snapshot end'
                        else 'i.' || quote_ident(column_name) end, ', ' order by ordinal_position)
      into v_cols, v_cols_i
      from information_schema.columns where table_schema = 'public' and table_name = 'mock_exam_set_items' and is_generated = 'NEVER' and column_name not in ('id', 'exam_set_id', 'created_at');
    execute format('insert into mock_exam_set_items (exam_set_id, %s) select $1, %s from mock_exam_set_items i join problems p on p.id = i.problem_id where i.exam_set_id = $2', v_cols, v_cols_i)
      using v_new_set, v_src.id, v_pids;
    if v_src.format = 'mst' and not coalesce((public.mock_exam_validate_mst_set(v_new_set)->>'ready')::boolean, false) then
      raise exception 'New version of % is not ready (4-module blueprint check failed).', v_src.name;
    end if;
    select string_agg(x.problem_id::text || ':' || x.issue, ', ') into v_bad from (select * from public.mock_exam_set_item_issues(v_new_set) where issue is not null limit 5) x;
    if v_bad is not null then raise exception 'New version of % has item issues: %', v_src.name, v_bad; end if;
    perform public.mock_exam_publish_set(v_new_set, v_actor);
    v_done_s := v_done_s + 1;
    raise notice 'set % v% -> v% (%)', v_src.name, v_src.version_no, v_src.version_no + 1, v_new_set;
  end loop;
  raise notice 'DONE problems applied=% skipped=%; sets applied=% skipped=%', v_done_p, v_skip_p, v_done_s, v_skip_s;
end
$audit$;
